<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch7-secondorder">
	<title>Second-Order Circuits</title>

	<introduction>

		<p>Second-order circuits contain two equivalent energy storage elements. This could be two capacitors that cannot be combined to an equivalent capacitance, two inductors that cannot be combined to an equivalent inductance, or one equivalent capacitance and one equivalent inductance.</p>

		<p>In this chapter, these circuits will be analyzed given an abrupt change from one set of DC conditions to another. (There will be an exception to this when we briefly analyze non-constant input non-homogeneous second-order circuits in <xref ref="ch7-variableinput" />.) The short-term difference in output that occurs as a result of this change in source conditions is known as a <term>transient analysis</term>.</p>
		
		<p>On the other hand, a <term>steady-state analysis</term> considers the operation of a circuit when conditions remain steady for a long period of time. To analyze the behavior of a capacitor in a DC steady-state condition, recall from <xref ref="ch5-vicap" />:</p>

			<blockquote>
				<p>Based on <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />, if the voltage remains unchanging through time, there will be no current flowing through the branch that contains a capacitor. Therefore, in DC conditions, a capacitor can be replaced by an open circuit.</p>
			</blockquote>
		
		<p>To analyze the behavior of an inductor in a DC steady-state condition, recall from <xref ref="ch6-viinductance" />:</p>

			<blockquote>
				<p>Based on <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />, if the current remains unchanging through time, there will be no voltage drop over an inductor. Therefore, in DC conditions, an inductor can be replaced by a short circuit.</p>
			</blockquote>

		<p>Recall also from <xref ref="ch5-capacitors" /> and <xref ref="ch6-inductors" /> that the voltage drop over a capacitor and current through an inductor are both quantities that are continuous with time. That is, <m>i_L(t^-) = i_L(t^+)</m> and <m>v_C(t^-) = v_C(t^+)</m>. However, the voltage drop over an inductor and current through a capacitor may be discontinuous with time. These properties are important to keep in mind when calculating the initial conditions of a second-order circuit.</p>
		
		<p>An equation for voltage or current derived in a second-order circuit will represent a second-order differential equation. These differential equations can be classified in a few different ways.</p>
		
		<p>The differential equation may be <term>homogeneous</term> or <term>non-homogeneous</term>.</p>
		
		<p>The equation may represent a function that is <term>underdamped</term>, <term>overdamped</term>, or <term>critically damped</term>. The function type will dictate the solution to the differential equation. (These solutions will be presented, but not derived, in this textbook.)</p>
		
		<p>The procedure for analyzing the transient response of a homogeneous or constant-input non-homogeneous second-order circuit includes the following steps.</p>
		
		<p>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p>Derive the initial conditions of the circuit.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>Derive the final conditions of the circuit. (These will be equal to zero in homogeneous circuits.)</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>If the circuit fits into one of the two standard types (series RLC or parallel RLC), derive values for the damping parameter, resonant frequency, and initial first derivative using the standard equations.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>If the circuit does not fit into one of the two standard types, derive a differential equation corresponding to the desired circuit parameter. Use this differential equation to calculate the damping parameter, resonant frequency, and initial first derivative.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>Use the values of the damping parameter and resonant frequency to determine what type of circuit is represented (underdamped, overdamped, or critically damped). Follow the equation for the solution format to derive an equation for the desired circuit parameter.</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</p>

		<p>The procedure for analyzing a non-constant input non-homogeneous circuit will be described in <xref ref="ch7-variableinput" />.</p>
	
  	</introduction>

	<section xml:id="ch7-second-order-review">

		<title>Review of Important Mathematical Concepts for Second-Order Circuits</title>
			
		<p>The circuits described in this chapter represent second-order, linear circuits. Analysis of the circuits will yield a second-order, linear, ordinary differential equation with constant coefficients. </p>

		<p>
			<ul>
				<li><p>Second-order describes the highest-order derivative in the equation. A second-order equation has a second-derivative term and no higher-order derivatives.</p></li>
				<li><p>Linear describes the operations that occur on the function. If the operations are linear, then the equation is linear.</p></li>
				<li><p>Ordinary describes the types of derivatives in the equation. If there are no partial derivatives, then the equation is ordinary.</p></li>
			</ul>
		</p>

		<p>An example of a second-order, linear, ordinary differential equation with constant coefficients is shown in <xref ref="ch7-secondorderlinearode" />.</p>

		<p><md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-secondorderlinearode">a_2 \frac{d^2f(t)}{dt^2} + a_1 \frac{df(t)}{dt} + a_0 f(t) = y(t)</md></p>

		<p>In this textbook, we will derive equations in <term>standard form</term>, which means that the coefficient of the highest-order derivative term (<m>a_2</m> in <xref ref="ch7-secondorderlinearode" />) will be equal to one. When in standard form, second-order equations can be described as shown in <xref ref="ch7-secondorderlinearode-standard" />, where <m>\alpha</m> is known as the <term>damping parameter</term> of the circuit and <m>\omega_0</m> is known as the <term>resonant frequency</term> of the circuit. These two parameters will be described in greater detail in <xref ref="ch7-damping-types" />.</p>

		<p><md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-secondorderlinearode-standard">\frac{d^2f(t)}{dt^2} + 2\alpha\frac{df(t)}{dt} + \omega_0^2 f(t) = y(t)</md></p>

		<p>When the function <m>y(t)</m> as defined in <xref ref="ch7-secondorderlinearode-standard" /> is equal to zero, the equation is said to be <term>homogeneous</term>, otherwise the equation is non-homogeneous. This chapter will cover both constant input and non-constant input non-homogeneous circuits. However, for non-constant input non-homogeneous circuits, we will find that it is much easier to analyze them using the method introduced in <xref ref="ch11-laplace" />.</p>

		<p>In order to obtain the exact solution of a second-order, linear, ordinary differential equation, we will need to know the initial value of the function being described by the equation <alert>and the initial value of the first derivative of the function being described by the equation</alert>. Both of these values must be calculated at a time of <m>0^+</m>, which is important to keep in mind if a quantity is discontinuous with time. If the equation is non-homogeneous, we will also need to know the value of the particular solution.</p>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch7-damping-types">
		<title>Circuit Damping</title>
		
		<introduction>
			<p>As described in <xref ref="ch7-second-order-review" />, the standard form of the second-order differential equations derived in this textbook (described by <xref ref="ch7-secondorderlinearode" /> where <m>a_2 = 1</m>) will define two important circuit parameters.</p>
			
			<p>Coefficient <m>a_1</m> is used to derive the <term>damping parameter</term> (<m>\alpha</m>) of the circuit. This quantity describes how quickly the value of voltage or current responds to changes to source values. (If the source is disconnected or reduced, it describes how quickly the output will decay to its final steady-state value. If the source is connected or increased, it describes how quickly the output will rise to its final steady-state value.) The damping parameter is defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-damping-parameter" />. The unit of <m>\alpha</m> is Np/s (nepers per second). The neper is a dimensionless quantity similar to radians. It is named after John Napier, the inventor of the logarithm.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-damping-parameter">\alpha = \frac{a_1}{2}</md>
			</p>

			<remark>
				<title>Summary of the damping parameter</title>

				<p>
					<ul>
						<li><p>Symbol: <m>\alpha</m></p></li>
						<li><p>Unit: Np/s (nepers per second)</p></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</remark>

			<p>Coefficient <m>a_0</m> is used to derive the <term>resonant frequency</term> (<m>\omega_0</m>) of the circuit. The concept of resonance is outside the scope of this textbook. The resonant frequency describes the frequency at which the circuit would have a minimum impedance. (The term impedance will be defined in <xref ref="ch8-phasors" />. For now, it can be considered analogous to resistance.) The resonant frequency is defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-resonant-frequency" />. The unit of <m>\omega_0</m> is rad/s.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-resonant-frequency">\omega_0 = \sqrt{a_0}</md>
			</p>

			<remark>
				<title>Summary of the resonant frequency</title>

				<p>
					<ul>
						<li><p>Symbol: <m>\omega_0</m></p></li>
						<li><p>Unit: rad/s (radians per second)</p></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</remark>

			<p>When solving a differential equation, the <term>characteristic equation</term> describes the polynomial described by the coefficients of the differential equation. This characteristic equation will yield two roots, <m>s_1</m> and <m>s_2</m>, defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots2" />. These roots are not usually expressed with a unit. However, because they are multiplied by time in the exponent of an exponential function, it is important to understand that they are not dimensionless quantities.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-roots1">s_1 \amp= -\alpha + \sqrt{\alpha^2 - \omega_0^2}</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-roots2">s_2 \amp= -\alpha - \sqrt{\alpha^2 - \omega_0^2}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>All second-order circuits with a positive resistance will have negative roots, indicating a stable response to a change in conditions (all exponential terms will converge rather than diverge over time). (Circuit stability is discussed in <xref ref="ch9-funcpolezero" />.)</p>

			<p>Based on the value of the roots, there are three different possible output responses that a homogeneous second-order circuit can have. These are: overdamped, underdamped, and critically damped. Each will be explored below.</p>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-overdamped-type">
			<title>Overdamped</title>

			<p>An overdamped circuit response occurs when <m>\alpha \gt \omega_0</m>. In an overdamped circuit, the voltage or current takes a long time to respond to changes in the circuit conditions. Both of the roots of the characteristic equation (<m>s_1</m> and <m>s_2</m>) are real and unique.</p>

			<p>A graph depicting the voltage response of an overdamped circuit is shown in <xref ref="homogeneous-overdamped-graph" />. A similar graph could be made for the current in an overdamped circuit. The solid plot represents the source voltage, which abruptly changes from 5<nbsp />V to 0<nbsp />V at a time of zero seconds. (This represents a homogeneous circuit.) The dashed line represents an overdamped output voltage corresponding to that change.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="homogeneous-overdamped-graph">
  				<caption>Voltage response of an overdamped second-order circuit to an abrupt removal of source voltage.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-overdamped-graph">
					<shortdescription>Graph with time (arbitrary unit) on the x-axis and voltage (V) on the y-axis. In thick black is a function starting at 5V and changing to 0V at a time of 0 and staying 0. The dashed line shows a value of 5V until 0 and then a gradual decay to 0 over time.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\begin{axis}[xlabel=$t$ (arbitrary), ylabel=voltage (V), xmin=-10, xmax=50, ymin=-0.5, ymax=5.5, height=5cm, width=\linewidth, legend style={cells={anchor=west}, legend pos=north east}]
								\addplot[very thick, black] coordinates {(-10,5) (0,5) (0,0) (50,0)};
								\addlegendentry{$V_{IN}$}
								\addplot[black, thick, dashed, domain=0:50, samples=100] {5.2*exp(-0.1*x)-0.2*exp(-1.8*x)};
								\addlegendentry{$v(t)$}
	    					\end{axis}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>
		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-underdamped-type">
			<title>Underdamped</title>

			<p>An underdamped circuit response occurs when <m>\alpha \lt \omega_0</m>. In an underdamped circuit, the voltage or current responds so quickly to changes in source conditions that the output oscillates around the final value, eventually attenuating to zero. The roots of the characteristic equation (<m>s_1</m> and <m>s_2</m>) are complex conjugates (recall that complex conjugates have the form of <m>a \pm jb</m>, where <m>j = \sqrt{-1}</m>).</p>

			<p>The <term>oscillation frequency</term> (<m>\beta</m>) describes the frequency at which the circuit parameter will oscillate. The oscillation frequency is defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-oscillation-freq" />. The unit of the oscillation frequency is rad/s (radians per second).</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-oscillation-freq">\beta = \sqrt{\omega_0^2 - \alpha^2}</md>
			</p>

			<remark>
				<title>Summary of the oscillation frequency</title>

				<p>
					<ul>
						<li><p>Symbol: <m>\beta</m></p></li>
						<li><p>Unit: rad/s (radians per second)</p></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</remark>
			
			<p>A graph depicting the voltage response of an underdamped circuit is shown in <xref ref="homogeneous-underdamped-graph" />. A similar graph could be made for the current in an underdamped circuit. The solid plot represents the source voltage, which abruptly changes from 5<nbsp />V to 0<nbsp />V at a time of zero seconds. (This represents a homogeneous circuit.) The dashed line represents an underdamped output voltage corresponding to that change.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="homogeneous-underdamped-graph">
  				<caption>Voltage response of an underdamped second-order circuit to an abrupt removal of source voltage.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-underdamped-overdamped-graph">
					<shortdescription>Graph with time (arbitrary unit) on the x-axis and voltage (V) on the y-axis. In thick black is a function starting at 5V and changing to 0V at a time of 0 and staying 0. The dashed line shows a value of 5V until 0 and then a sinusoidal oscillation decaying to 0 over time.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\begin{axis}[xlabel=$t$ (arbitrary), ylabel=voltage (V), xmin=-10, xmax=50, ymin=-5.5, ymax=5.5, height=5cm, width=\linewidth, legend style={cells={anchor=west}, legend pos=north east}]
								\addplot[very thick, black] coordinates {(-10,5) (0,5) (0,0) (50,0)};
								\addlegendentry{$V_{IN}$}
								\addplot[thick, black, dashed, domain=0:50, samples=100] {exp(-0.05*x)*(5*cos(deg(x))-3*sin(deg(x)))};
								\addlegendentry{$v(t)$}
	    					\end{axis}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>
		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-critically-damped-type">
			<title>Critically Damped</title>

			<p>A critically damped circuit response occurs when <m>\alpha = \omega_0</m>. The critically damped circuit has the quickest approach to the steady-state value without oscillating. (In other words, it is the boundary condition between an overdamped and an underdamped circuit.) Both of the roots of the characteristic equation (<m>s_1</m> and <m>s_2</m>) are real and equal.</p>

			<p>A graph depicting the voltage response of a critically damped circuit is shown in <xref ref="homogeneous-critically-damped-graph" />. A similar graph could be made for the current in a critically damped circuit. The solid plot represents the source voltage, which abruptly changes from 5<nbsp />V to 0<nbsp />V at a time of zero seconds. (This represents a homogeneous circuit.) The dashed line represents a critically damped output voltage corresponding to that change.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="homogeneous-critically-damped-graph">
  				<caption>Voltage response of a critically-damped second-order circuit to an abrupt removal of source voltage.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-critically-damped-graph">
					<shortdescription>Graph with time (arbitrary unit) on the x-axis and voltage (V) on the y-axis. In thick black is a function starting at 5V and changing to 0V at a time of 0 and staying 0. The dashed line shows a value of 5V until 0 and then a decay to 0 over time.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\begin{axis}[xlabel=$t$ (arbitrary), ylabel=voltage (V), xmin=-10, xmax=50, ymin=-0.5, ymax=5.5, height=5cm, width=\linewidth, legend style={cells={anchor=west}, legend pos=north east}]
								\addplot[very thick, black] coordinates {(-10,5) (0,5) (0,0) (50,0)};
								\addlegendentry{$V_{IN}$}
								\addplot[thick, black, dashed, domain=0:50, samples=100] {5*exp(-0.4*x) + 1*x*exp(-0.4*x)};
								\addlegendentry{$v(t)$}
	    					\end{axis}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>
		</subsection>
	
	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch7-the-series-rlc-circuit">
		<title>The Series RLC Circuit</title>

		<introduction>
			<p>A common form of second-order circuit is the series RLC circuit. A series RLC circuit is configured such that, in the final configuration of the circuit after a change to the source conditions is made, the circuit components contain one or more resistor, capacitor, and inductor, all in series with each other. In this section, we'll analyze both homogeneous and non-homogeneous series RLC circuits to derive their common properties. We'll see that, regardless of if a circuit is homogeneous or not, properties such as the damping parameter and resonant frequency will be the same for all series RLC circuits.</p>

			<p>An example of a series RLC circuit is shown in <xref ref="seriesrlc-diagram" />.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="seriesrlc-diagram">
  				<caption>A series RLC circuit.</caption>
				<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-seriesrlc-diagram">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a voltage source Vin, SPDT switch, resistor R, inductor L, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of Vin connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. The low-potential end of Vin, position b of the SPDT switch, and C connect at node b. The SPDT input and R connect at node c. R and L connect at node d. L and C connect at node e. Voltage v(t) is defined over C from node d to node b. Branch current i(t) is defined through C from node d to node b. The SPDT switch is depicted as being at position a.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) node[cute spdt up, xscale=-1, anchor=in] (switch) {};
							\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[above] {a} to [short] ++ (left:2) to [V, l_=$V_{IN}$] ++(down:2.75) |- (7.5,0);
							\draw[]	(switch.in) to [R, l^=$R$]	++(right:2.5);
							\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(2.5,0)$) to [L, l^=$L$]	++(right:2.5);
							\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$] (7.5,0);
							\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[below] {b}  to [short] ++(left:0.25) to [short, -*] ++(down:2.08);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Note that an inductor including a parasitic resistance will not affect the configuration of this circuit, as that resistance will be in series with all other circuit elements.</p>
		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-homoseriesrlc">
			<title>Homogeneous Series RLC Circuits</title>

			<p>Consider the series RLC circuit shown in <xref ref="seriesrlc-diagram" />. The homogeneous condition considers that the switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at time <m>t=0</m>.</p>

			<p>Kirchhoff's laws will be used to derive a second-order differential equation for this circuit. With the switch in position b, the circuit resembles that shown in <xref ref="homogeneous-seriesrlc-diagram-t0plus" />.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="homogeneous-seriesrlc-diagram-t0plus">
  				<caption>The series RLC circuit shown in <xref ref="seriesrlc-diagram" /> after the switch has been moved to position b.</caption>
				<image width="50%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-seriesrlc-diagram-t0plus">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with resistor R, capacitor C, and inductor L all connected in series. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor and branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short] (0,0) to [short]	(5,0);
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$] (2.5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$] (5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$] (5,0);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>First, perform KVL around the loop. The voltage dropped over the resistor is defined by Ohm's law and the voltage dropped over the inductor is defined by <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />. Because all three circuit elements are in series, they share a common current <m>i(t)</m>. The KVL equation is described in <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-seriesrlc-kvl" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-seriesrlc-kvl" >Ri(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + v(t) = 0</md>
			</p>

			<p>For a second equation, the voltage over and current through the capacitor must be related. Fortunately, we already have an equation that describes this relationship: <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />.</p>
			
			<p>Plug <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" /> into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-seriesrlc-kvl" /> and simplify to standard form. The result is shown in <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-seriesrlc-diffeq" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow>0 \amp= RC \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + L\frac{d}{dt}\left(C \frac{dv(t)}{dt}\right) + v(t)</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= LC \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + RC \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-seriesrlc-diffeq">\amp= \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{R}{L} \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{LC}v(t)</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Referring to <xref ref="ch7-secondorderlinearode-standard" />, we can easily derive values for the damping parameter and resonant frequency of a series RLC circuit. These are defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-alpha" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-omega0" />, respectively.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-alpha">\alpha \amp= \frac{R}{2L}</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-omega0">\omega_0 \amp= \frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Another important quantity that must be known to solve a second-order circuit is the first derivative of the voltage or current, solved at a time of <m>t=0^+</m>. We therefore must analyze <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-seriesrlc-kvl" /> and <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" /> to see if either one contains a first derivative of the voltage. This is defined in <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />. We can therefore solve this at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the first derivative initial condition value for a series RLC circuit. This value is described in <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-vpzero" />. (It is vitally important to point out that this first derivative initial condition is <alert>only valid for series RLC circuits</alert>!)</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow>i(0^+) \amp= Cv'(0^+)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-vpzero">v'(0^+) \amp= \frac{i(0^+)}{C}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-non-homoseriesrlc">
			<title>Non-Homogeneous Series RLC Circuits</title>

			<p>Consider the series RLC circuit shown in <xref ref="seriesrlc-diagram" />. The non-homogeneous condition considers that the switch is in position b for a very long time before moving to position a at time <m>t=0</m>.</p>

			<p>Kirchhoff's laws will be used to derive a second-order differential equation for this circuit. With the switch in position b, the circuit resembles that shown in <xref ref="non-homogeneous-seriesrlc-diagram-t0plus" />.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="non-homogeneous-seriesrlc-diagram-t0plus">
  				<caption>The series RLC circuit shown in <xref ref="seriesrlc-diagram" /> after the switch has been moved to position a.</caption>
				<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-seriesrlc-diagram-t0plus">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with voltage source Vin, resistor R, capacitor C, and inductor L all connected in series. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor and branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{IN}$] (0,0) to [short]	(5,0);
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$] (2.5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$] (5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$] (5,0);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>First, perform KVL around the loop. The voltage dropped over the resistor is defined by Ohm's law and the voltage dropped over the inductor is defined by <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />. Because all four circuit elements are in series, they share a common current <m>i(t)</m>. The KVL equation is described in <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-seriesrlc-kvl" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-seriesrlc-kvl" >Ri(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + v(t) = V_{IN}</md>
			</p>

			<p>For a second equation, the voltage over and current through the capacitor must be related. Fortunately, we already have an equation that describes this relationship: <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />.</p>
			
			<p>Plug <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" /> into <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-seriesrlc-kvl" /> and simplify to standard form. The result is shown in <xref ref="ch7-non-homogeneous-seriesrlc-diffeq" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow>V_{IN} \amp= RC \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + L\frac{d}{dt}\left(C \frac{dv(t)}{dt}\right) + v(t)</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= LC \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + RC \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-seriesrlc-diffeq">\frac{V_{IN}}{LC} \amp= \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{R}{L} \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{LC}v(t)</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Referring to <xref ref="ch7-secondorderlinearode-standard" />, we can see that the values for the damping parameter and resonant frequency are identical to that of a homogeneous series RLC circuit. In addition, because <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" /> is still the equation that describes the first derivative of voltage, the initial condition of the first derivative continues to be defined by <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-vpzero" />.</p>

		</subsection>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch7-the-parallel-rlc-circuit">
		<title>The Parallel RLC Circuit</title>

		<introduction>
			<p>A common form of second-order circuit is the parallel RLC circuit. A parallel RLC circuit is configured such that, in the final configuration of the circuit after a change to the source conditions is made, the circuit components contain one or more equivalent resistor, capacitor, and inductor, all in parallel with each other. In this section, we'll analyze both homogeneous and non-homogeneous parallel RLC circuits to derive their common properties. We'll see that, regardless of if a circuit is homogeneous or not, properties such as the damping parameter and resonant frequency will be the same for all parallel RLC circuits.</p>

			<p>An example of a parallel RLC circuit is shown in <xref ref="parallelrlc-diagram" />.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="parallelrlc-diagram">
  				<caption>A parallel RLC circuit.</caption>
				<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-parallelrlc-diagram">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of current source Iin, SPDT switch, resistor R, inductor L, and capacitor C. Iin connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. Iin flows into node a. Position b of the SPDT switch is floating at node b. The input of the SPDT switch, R, L, and C connect at node c. Iin, R, L, and C connect at node d. Iin flows out of node d. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node c to node d. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node c to node d. The SPDT switch is depicted as being in position a.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) node[cute spdt up, xscale=-1, anchor=in] (switch) {};
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_{IN}$]							++(up:2.75);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) --++ (right:0.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*]		++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, i^=$i(t)$, *-*]			++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]					++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[above] {a}						 -| (0,2.75);
							\node[below]	at (switch.out 2) {b};
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(8,0);
							\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [short]									(8,2.5);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>
		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-homoparallelrlc">
			<title>Homogeneous Parallel RLC Circuits</title>

			<p>Consider the parallel RLC circuit shown in <xref ref="parallelrlc-diagram" />. The homogeneous condition considers that the switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at time <m>t=0</m>.</p>

			<p>Kirchhoff's laws will be used to derive a second-order differential equation for this circuit. With the switch in position b, the circuit resembles that shown in <xref ref="homogeneous-parallelrlc-diagram-t0plus" />.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="homogeneous-parallelrlc-diagram-t0plus">
  				<caption>The parallel RLC circuit shown in <xref ref="parallelrlc-diagram" /> after the switch has been moved to position b.</caption>
				<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-diagram-t0plus">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with two nodes consisting of resistor R, capacitor C, and inductor L, all connected in parallel. Branch current i(t) flows through L. Voltage v(t) drops over C.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$]						++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, i^=$i(t)$, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]			++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>First, perform KCL at either node. The current through over the resistor is defined by Ohm's law and the current through the capacitor is defined by <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />. Because all three circuit elements are in parallel, they share a common voltage <m>v(t)</m>. The KCL equation is described in <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-parallelrlc-kcl" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-parallelrlc-kcl" >\frac{v(t)}{R} + i(t) + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} = 0</md>
			</p>

			<p>For a second equation, the voltage over and current through the inductor must be related. Fortunately, we already have an equation that describes this relationship: <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />.</p>
			
			<p>Plug <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-parallelrlc-kcl" /> and simplify to standard form. The result is shown in <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-diffeq" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow>0 \amp= \frac{1}{R}L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + i(t) + C\frac{d}{dt}\left(L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right)</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= LC\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{L}{R}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + i(t)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-diffeq">\amp= \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{1}{RC}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{LC}i(t)</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Referring to <xref ref="ch7-secondorderlinearode-standard" />, we can easily derive values for the damping parameter and resonant frequency of a parallel RLC circuit. These are defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-alpha" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-omega0" />, respectively.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-alpha">\alpha \amp= \frac{1}{2RC}</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-omega0">\omega_0 \amp= \frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Another important quantity that must be known to solve a second-order circuit is the first derivative of the voltage or current, solved at a time of <m>t=0^+</m>. We therefore must analyze <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-parallelrlc-kcl" /> and <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> to see if either one contains a first derivative of the current. This is defined in <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />. We can therefore solve this at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the first derivative initial condition value for a parallel RLC circuit. This value is described in <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-ipzero" />. (It is vitally important to point out that this first derivative initial condition is <alert>only valid for parallel RLC circuits</alert>!)</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= Li'(0^+)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-ipzero">i'(0^+) \amp= \frac{v(0^+)}{L}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-non-homoparallelrlc">
			<title>Non-Homogeneous Parallel RLC Circuits</title>

			<p>Consider the parallel RLC circuit shown in <xref ref="parallelrlc-diagram" />. The non-homogeneous condition considers that the switch is in position b for a very long time before moving to position a at time <m>t=0</m>.</p>

			<p>Kirchhoff's laws will be used to derive a second-order differential equation for this circuit. With the switch in position b, the circuit resembles that shown in <xref ref="non-homogeneous-parallelrlc-diagram-t0plus" />.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="non-homogeneous-parallelrlc-diagram-t0plus">
  				<caption>The parallel RLC circuit shown in <xref ref="parallelrlc-diagram" /> after the switch has been moved to position a.</caption>
				<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-parallelrlc-diagram-t0plus">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with two nodes consisting of current source Iin, resistor R, capacitor C, and inductor L, all connected in parallel. Branch current i(t) flows through L. Voltage v(t) drops over C.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_{IN}$]					++(up:2.5);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*]				++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, i^=$i(t)$, *-*]		++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]			++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(7.5,2.5);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>First, perform KCL at either node. The current through over the resistor is defined by Ohm's law and the current through the capacitor is defined by <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />. Because all four circuit elements are in parallel, they share a common voltage <m>v(t)</m>. The KCL equation is described in <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-parallelrlc-kcl" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-parallelrlc-kcl" >\frac{v(t)}{R} + i(t) + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} = I_{IN}</md>
			</p>

			<p>For a second equation, the voltage over and current through the inductor must be related. Fortunately, we already have an equation that describes this relationship: <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />.</p>
			
			<p>Plug <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> into <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-parallelrlc-kcl" /> and simplify to standard form. The result is shown in <xref ref="ch7-non-homogeneous-parallelrlc-diffeq" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow>I_{IN} \amp= \frac{1}{R}L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + i(t) + C\frac{d}{dt}\left(L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right)</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= LC\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{L}{R}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + i(t)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-parallelrlc-diffeq">\frac{I_{IN}}{LC} \amp= \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{1}{RC}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{LC}i(t)</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Referring to <xref ref="ch7-secondorderlinearode-standard" />, we can see that the values for the damping parameter and resonant frequency are identical to that of a homogeneous parallel RLC circuit. In addition, because <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> is still the equation that describes the first derivative of current, the initial condition of the first derivative continues to be defined by <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-ipzero" />.</p>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-parallel-rlc-parasitic-resistance">
			<title>Analysis with Parasitic Resistance</title>

			<p>The analyses above ignores the parasitic resistance that exists in real inductors. A realistic circuit, which explicitly includes the parasitic resistance in series with the inductor, can be analyzed. The corresponding circuit diagram is shown in <xref ref="parallelrlc-parasitic-resistance-general" />.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="parallelrlc-parasitic-resistance-general">
  				<caption>Parallel RLC circuit with parasitic resistance.</caption>
				<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-parallelrlc-parasitic-resistance-general">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of current source Iin, SPDT switch, resistor R, resistor Rp, inductor L, and capacitor C. Iin connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. Iin flows into node a. Position b of the SPDT switch is floating at node b. The input of the SPDT switch, R, L, and C connect at node c. L and Rp connect at node e. Iin, R, Rp, and C connect at node d. Iin flows out of node d. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node c to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node c to node d. The SPDT switch is depicted as being in position a.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(2.5,5) node[cute spdt up, xscale=-1, anchor=in] (switch) {};
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_{IN}$]				++(up:5.25);
							\draw[]	(2.5,5) --++ (right:0.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*]	++(down:5);
							\draw[]	(5.5,5) to [L, l_=$L$, i^=$i(t)$, *-]	++(down:2.5) to [R, l_=$R_P$, -*]	++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(8,5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]			++(down:5);
							\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[above] {a}			-| (0,2.75);
							\node[below]	at (switch.out 2) {b};
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(8,0);
							\draw[]	(3,5) to [short]						(8,5);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Consider the circuit with the switch having moved to position b at a time of zero seconds, depicted in <xref ref="homogeneous-parallelrlc-parasitic-diagram-t0plus" />. This analysis corresponds to the homogeneous circuit. Hopefully by now you realize that, whether the circuit is homogeneous or not, the initial condition of the first derivative, damping parameter, and oscillation frequency will be defined by the same equations. Therefore, a separate analysis will not be performed for the non-homogeneous circuit.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="homogeneous-parallelrlc-parasitic-diagram-t0plus">
  				<caption>The parallel RLC circuit shown in <xref ref="parallelrlc-parasitic-resistance-general" /> after the switch has been moved to position b.</caption>
				<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-parasitic-diagram-t0plus">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes consisting of resistor R, resistor Rp, inductor L, and capacitor C. R, L, and C connect at node a. L and Rp connect at node b. R, Rp, and C connect at node c. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node a to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l_=$R$]					++(down:5);
							\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=$L$, i^=$i(t)$, *-]	++(down:2.5) to [R, l_=$R_P$, -*]	++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(5,5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]			++(down:5);
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
							\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]						(5,5);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>KCL can be used to find the first equation relating the circuit parameters, which is identical to that of the non-parasitic RLC circuit, defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-parallelrlc-kcl" />.</p>
			
			<p>Next, perform KVL around the loop containing the inductor, parasitic resistance, and capacitor. This is shown in <xref ref="ch7-eq-parasiticrlc-kvl" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-parasiticrlc-kvl">v(t) = L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_Pi(t)</md>
			</p>
			
			<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-eq-parasiticrlc-kvl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-parallelrlc-kcl" /> and derive the second-order differential equation in standard form. The final result is shown in <xref ref="ch7-eq-parasiticrlc-diffeq" /> and derived in <xref ref="ch7-parasiticrlc-diffeq-derivation" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-parasiticrlc-diffeq">0 = \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{1}{RC} + \frac{R_P}{L}\right]\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{1}{LC}+\frac{R_P}{RLC}\right]i(t)</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch7-parasiticrlc-diffeq-derivation">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>0 \amp= \frac{1}{R}\left[L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_Pi(t)\right] + i(t) + C\frac{d}{dt}\left[L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_Pi(t)\right]</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{L}{R}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{R_P}{R}i(t) + i(t) + LC\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + CR_P\frac{di(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= LC\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{L}{R} + CR_P\right]\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{R_P}{R} + 1\right]i(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{1}{RC} + \frac{R_P}{L}\right]\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{1}{LC}+\frac{R_P}{RLC}\right]i(t)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>Referring to <xref ref="ch7-secondorderlinearode-standard" />, we can easily derive values for the damping parameter and resonant frequency of a parallel RLC circuit with parasitic resistance. These are defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-alpha" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-omega0" />, respectively.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-alpha">\alpha \amp= \frac{1}{2RC}+\frac{R_P}{2L}</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-omega0">\omega_0 \amp= \sqrt{\frac{1}{LC}+\frac{R_P}{RLC}}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Another important quantity that must be known to solve a second-order circuit is the first derivative of the voltage or current, solved at a time of <m>t=0^+</m>. We therefore must analyze <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-parallelrlc-kcl" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-parasiticrlc-kvl" /> to see if either one contains a first derivative of the current. This is defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-parasiticrlc-kvl" />. We can therefore solve this at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the first derivative initial condition value for a parallel RLC circuit with parasitic resistance. This value is described in <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-ipzero" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= Li'(0^+) - R_Pi(0^+)</mrow>
					<mrow>Li'(0^+) \amp= v(0^+) + R_Pi(0^+)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-ipzero">i'(0^+) \amp= \frac{v(0^+)-R_Pi(0^+)}{L}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>
			
		</subsection>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch7-secondorderresponses">
		<title>Homogeneous Second-Order Circuits</title>

		<introduction>
		
			<p>The solution to a homogeneous second-order differential equation is known as the <term>general solution</term>.</p>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-overdamped-solutions">
			<title>The Overdamped Solution</title>

			<p>The solution to a homogeneous overdamped circuit is described in <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over" />, with the equation coefficients <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m> defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A2" />, respectively. Note that this equation is defined with voltage terms. If calculating current, all <m>v(t)</m> terms can be replaced by <m>i(t)</m> terms.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over">v(t) \amp= \left(A_1\textrm{e}^{s_1t} + A_2\textrm{e}^{s_2t}\right)u(t) + v(0^-)u(-t)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A1">A_1 \amp= \frac{v(0^+)s_2-v'(0^+)}{s_2-s_1}</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A2">A_2 \amp= \frac{v'(0^+)-v(0^+)s_1}{s_2-s_1}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Coefficients <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m> will have a unit corresponding to the quantity being described by the equation. (Volts for voltage, amps or milliamps for current.) These two coefficients are derived by solving <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over" /> at <m>t=0^+</m> and by solving the first derivative of <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over" /> at <m>t=0^+</m>. This derivation is shown in <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-overdamped-coefficient-derivation" />.</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-overdamped-coefficient-derivation">
				<p>Start by solving <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over" /> when <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= A_1\textrm{e}^{s_1*0} + A_2\textrm{e}^{s_2*0}</mrow>
						<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= A_1 + A_2</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Calculate the first derivative of <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over" /> and solve when <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\frac{d}{dt}v(t) \amp= \frac{d}{dt}\left(A_1\textrm{e}^{s_1t} + A_2\textrm{e}^{s_2t}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(t) \amp= s_1A_1\textrm{e}^{s_1t} + s_2A_2\textrm{e}^{s_2t}</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= s_1A_1\textrm{e}^{s_1*0} + s_2A_2\textrm{e}^{s_2*0}</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= s_1A_1 + s_2A_2</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Use the equations for <m>v(0^+)</m> and <m>v'(0^+)</m> to solve for <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>A_1 \amp= v(0^+) - A_2</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= s_1(v(0^+)-A_2) + s_2A_2</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= s_1v(0^+) - A_2s_1 + A_2s_2</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) - s_1v(0^+) \amp= A_2 (s_2 - s_1)</mrow>
						<mrow>A_2 \amp= \frac{v'(0^+) - s_1v(0^+)}{s_2-s_1}</mrow>
						<mrow>A_1 \amp= v(0^+) - \left(\frac{v'(0^+) - s_1v(0^+)}{s_2-s_1}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>A_1(s_2-s_1) \amp= v(0^+)(s_2-s_1) - v'(0^+) + s_1v(0^+)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= v(0^+)s_2 - v(0^+)s_1 - v'(0^+) + s_1v(0^+)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= v(0^+)s_2 - v'(0^+)</mrow>
						<mrow>A_1 \amp= \frac{v(0^+)s_2 - v'(0^+)}{s_2-s_1}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-underdamped-solutions">
			<title>The Underdamped Solution</title>

			<p>The solution to a homogeneous underdamped circuit is described in <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under" />, with the equation coefficients <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m> defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under-B1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under-B2" />, respectively. Note that this equation is defined with voltage terms. If calculating current, all <m>v(t)</m> terms can be replaced by <m>i(t)</m> terms.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under">v(t) \amp= \textrm{e}^{-\alpha t} \left[ B_1\cos{\beta t} + B_2\sin{\beta t} \right]u(t) + v(0^-)u(-t)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under-B1">B_1 \amp= v(0^+)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under-B2">B_2 \amp= \frac{v'(0^+)+\alpha v(0^+)}{\beta}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Coefficients <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m> will have a unit corresponding to the quantity being described by the equation. (Volts for voltage, amps or milliamps for current.) These two coefficients are derived by solving <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under" /> at <m>t=0^+</m> and by solving the first derivative of <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under" /> at <m>t=0^+</m>. This derivation is shown in <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-underdamped-coefficient-derivation" />.</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-underdamped-coefficient-derivation">
				<p>Start by solving <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under" /> when <m>t=0^+</m>. This conveniently derives coefficient <m>B_1</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= \textrm{e}^{-\alpha*0} \left[ B_1\cos{(\beta *0)} + B_2\sin{(\beta *0)} \right]</mrow>
						<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= B_1</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Calculate the first derivative of <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under" /> and solve when <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\frac{d}{dt}v(t) \amp= \frac{d}{dt}\left(\textrm{e}^{-\alpha t} \left[ B_1\cos{\beta t} + B_2\sin{\beta t} \right]\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(t) \amp= \textrm{e}^{-\alpha t}\left[(\cos{\beta t})(B_2\beta - \alpha B_1) - (\sin{\beta t})(\alpha B_2 + \beta B_1)\right]</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= \textrm{e}^{-\alpha *0}\left[(\cos{(\beta *0)})(B_2\beta - \alpha B_1) - (\sin{(\beta *0)})(\alpha B_2 + \beta B_1)\right]</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= B_2\beta - \alpha B_1</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Use the equations for <m>v(0^+)</m> and <m>v'(0^+)</m> to solve for <m>B_2</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= B_2\beta - \alpha B_1</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) + \alpha B_1 \amp= B_2\beta</mrow>
						<mrow>B_2 \amp= \frac{v'(0^+) + \alpha B_1}{\beta}</mrow>
						<mrow>B_2 \amp= \frac{v'(0^+) + \alpha v(0^+)}{\beta}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-critically-solutions">
			<title>The Critically Damped Solution</title>

			<p>The solution to a homogeneous critically damped circuit is described in <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical" />, with the equation coefficients <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m> defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical-A2" />, respectively. Note that this equation is defined with voltage terms. If calculating current, all <m>v(t)</m> terms can be replaced by <m>i(t)</m> terms.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical">v(t) \amp= \left(A_1\textrm{e}^{-\alpha t} + A_2t\textrm{e}^{-\alpha t}\right)u(t) + v(0^-)u(-t)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical-A1">A_1 \amp= v(0^+)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical-A2">A_2 \amp= v'(0^+) + \alpha v(0^+)</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Coefficient <m>A_1</m> has a unit corresponding to the quantity being described by the equation. (Volts for voltage, amps or milliamps for current.) Coefficient <m>A_2</m> has a unit corresponding to the first derivative of the quantity being described by the equation (volts per second for voltage, or amps per second or milliamps per second for current). These two coefficients are derived by solving <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical" /> at <m>t=0^+</m> and by solving the first derivative of <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical" /> at <m>t=0^+</m>. This derivation is shown in <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-critical-coefficient-derivation" />.</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-critical-coefficient-derivation">
				<p>Start by solving <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical" /> when <m>t=0^+</m>. This conveniently derives coefficient <m>A_1</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= A_1\textrm{e}^{-\alpha *0} + A_2t\textrm{e}^{-\alpha *0}</mrow>
						<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= A_1</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Calculate the first derivative of <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical" /> and solve when <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\frac{d}{dt}v(t) \amp= \frac{d}{dt}\left(A_1\textrm{e}^{-\alpha t} + A_2t\textrm{e}^{-\alpha t}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(t) \amp= -\alpha A_1\textrm{e}^{-\alpha t} + \textrm{e}^{-\alpha t}(A_2 - A_2\alpha t)</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= -\alpha A_1\textrm{e}^{-\alpha *0} + \textrm{e}^{-\alpha *0}(A_2 - A_2\alpha *0)</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= -\alpha A_1 + A_2</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Use the equations for <m>v(0^+)</m> and <m>v'(0^+)</m> to solve for <m>A_2</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= -\alpha A_1 + A_2</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) + \alpha A_1 \amp= A_2</mrow>
						<mrow>A_2 \amp= v'(0^+) + \alpha v(0^+)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-examples">
			<title>Examples</title>

			<example>
				<title>Homogeneous RLC series circuit</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown below. The circuit is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-seriesrlc-example-fig1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 20V source, SPST switch, three resistors (100ohm, 220ohm, and 47ohm), inductor (50mH), and two capacitors (both 470nF). The high-potential end of the 20V source connects to the 100ohm resistor at node a. The 100ohm resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The SPST switch, 50mH inductor, and 47ohm resistor connect at node c. The 50mH inductor and 220ohm resistor connect at node d. The 47ohm resistor and both 470nF capacitors connect at node e. The low-potential end of the 20V source, 220ohm resistor, and both 470nF capacitors connect at node f. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node d. Voltage v(t) drops from node e to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=20~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$]				(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [cute opening switch]			(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [L, l_=50~mH, *-, i^=$i_L(t)$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=220~$\Omega$, -*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=47~$\Omega$]				(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [C, l_=470~nF, *-*]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [short]							(10,5);
									\draw[]	(10,5) to [C, l_=470~nF, v^=$v(t)$]			(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>First, simplify the circuit by combining the two capacitors in parallel.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-seriesrlc-example-fig2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 20V source, SPST switch, three resistors (100ohm, 220ohm, and 47ohm), inductor (50mH), and one capacitor (940nF). The high-potential end of the 20V source connects to the 100ohm resistor at node a. The 100ohm resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The SPST switch, 50mH inductor, and 47ohm resistor connect at node c. The 50mH inductor and 220ohm resistor connect at node d. The 47ohm resistor and the 940nF capacitor connect at node e. The low-potential end of the 20V source, 220ohm resistor, and the 940nF capacitor connect at node f. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 940nF capacitor from node e to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=20~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$]				(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [cute opening switch]			(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [L, l_=50~mH, *-, i^=$i_L(t)$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=220~$\Omega$, -*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=47~$\Omega$]				(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [C, l_=940~nF, v^=$v(t)$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the initial steady-state conditions of the circuit. The inductor can be treated as a short and the capacitor can be treated as an open.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-seriesrlc-example-fig3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a 20V source, 100ohm resistor, 220ohm resistor, and 47ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 20V source connects to the 100ohm resistor at node a. The 100ohm, 220ohm, and 47ohm resistors connect at node b. The 47ohm resistor floats at node c. The low-potential end of the 20V source and 220ohm resistor connect at node d. Branch current iL(0) flows through the 220ohm resistor from node b to node d. Voltage v(0) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]									(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=220~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$i_L(0)$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=47~$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the initial current through the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i_L(0) \amp= \frac{20\textrm{V}}{320~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 62.5~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate the initial voltage drop over the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= 20~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{220~\Omega}{320~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 13.75~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-seriesrlc-example-fig4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a 50mH inductor, 220ohm resistor, 47ohm resistor, and 940nF capacitor. The 50mH inductor and 47ohm resistor connect at node a. The 47ohm resistor and 940nF capacitor connect at node b. The 940nF capacitor and 220ohm resistor connect at node c. The 50mH inductor and 220ohm resistor connect at node d. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [L, l_=50~mH, i^=$i_L(t)$]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=220~$\Omega$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=47~$\Omega$]				(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [C, l_=940~nF, v^=$v(t)$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Reduce the circuit by combining the two series resistors.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-seriesrlc-example-fig5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of a 50mH inductor, 267ohm resistor, and 940nF capacitor. The 50mH inductor and 267ohm resistor connect at node a. The 267ohm resistor and 940nF capacitor connect at node b. The 940nF capacitor and 50mH inductor connect at node c. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l_=50~mH, i^=$i_L(t)$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=267~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=940~nF, v^=$v(t)$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Because the capacitor and inductor are in series in this circuit configuration, the current through the capacitor at <m>t=0^+</m> will be equal to the negative value of the current through the inductor at <m>t=0</m>. (The value is negative because the current directions are opposing. Current through the capacitor is defined as flowing from high to low potential.)</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-vpzero" /> to calculate the initial first derivative of the voltage. Use base units (farads and amps) to ensure that the result has a unit of V/s.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= \frac{-0.0625~\textrm{A}}{940\times10^{-9}~\textrm{F}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -66489.36~\textrm{V/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-alpha" /> to calculate the damping parameter. Use base units (henrys, ohms) to ensure that the result has a unit of Np/s.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{267~\Omega}{2\times0.05~\textrm{H}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2670~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-omega0" /> to calculate the resonant frequency. Use base units (henrys, farads) to ensure that the result has a unit of rad/s.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \frac{1}{\sqrt{(0.05~\textrm{H})(940\times10^{-9}~\textrm{F})}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4612.66~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is underdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-oscillation-freq" /> to calculate the oscillation frequency of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\beta \amp= \sqrt{4612.66^2 - 2670^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3761.35~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under-B1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under-B2" /> to calculate the coefficients.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>B_1 \amp= 13.75~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>B_2 \amp= \frac{-66489.36+(2670)(13.75)}{3761.35}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -7.92~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug all of these quantities into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under" /> to calculate the equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \textrm{e}^{-2670 t} \left[13.75~\textrm{V}~\cos(3761.35 t) -7.92~\textrm{V}~\sin(3761.35 t)	\right]~u(t) + 13.75~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>The voltage is plotted below as a function of time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-seriesrlc-example-fig6">
							<shortdescription>Graph of the equation defined above.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[width=\linewidth-20pt, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~($\mu$s), xmin=-100, xmax=1500, ylabel=$v(t)$~(V)]
										\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-100,13.75) (0,13.75)};
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:1500, samples=100] {exp(-.00267*x)*(13.75*cos(deg(.00376*x))-7.92*sin(deg(.00376*x)))};
									\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
				
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Homogeneous parallel RLC circuit</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit below. The switch is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-example-fig1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of a 6V source, 10ohm resistor, SPST switch, 30mH inductor, 300uF capacitor, and 5ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 6V source connects to the 10ohm resistor at node a. The 10ohm resistor and SPST switch connect at node b. The SPST switch, 30mH inductor, 330uF capacitor, and 5ohm resistor connect at node c. The low-potential end of the 6V source, 30mH inductor, 300uF capacitor, and 5ohm resistor connect at node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 5ohm resistor from node c to node d. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=6~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute opening switch]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=30~mH, *-*, i^=$i(t)$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]							(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=300~$\mu$F, *-*]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$]	(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in its initial steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short and the capacitor can be replaced by an open. Both <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> are continuous with time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-example-fig2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of a 6V source, 10ohm resistor, 5ohm resistor, and a short. The high-potential end of the 6V source connects to the 10ohm resistor at node a. The 10ohm resistor, 5ohm resistor, and short connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 6V source, short, and 5ohm resistor connect at node c. Branch current i(0) flows through the short from node b to node c. Voltage v(0) drops over the 5ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=6~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, *-*, i^=$i(0)$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$, v^=$v(0)$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 5<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is shorted out of the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-example-fig3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and two nodes consisting of a 6V source and 10ohm resistor. Both circuit elements are connected in series. Branch current i(0) flows through the circuit in the same direction as the voltage source. Voltage i(0) drops over a short.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=6~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(0)$, v^=$v(0)$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the initial current.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= \frac{6\textrm{V}}{10~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.6~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Because the initial voltage is measured over a short, the initial voltage drop will be zero. This means that <m>i'(0)</m> will also be equal to zero.</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-example-fig4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of a 30mH inductor, 300uF capacitor, and 5ohm resistor all connected in parallel. Voltage v(t) drops over all three circuit elements. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=30~mH, i^=$i(t)$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]							(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=300~$\mu$F, *-*]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$]	(10,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short]							(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-alpha" /> to calculate the damping parameter.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{1}{2(5~\Omega)(300\times10^{-6}~\textrm{F})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 333.33~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-omega0" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \frac{1}{\sqrt{(0.03~\textrm{H})(300\times10^{-6}~\textrm{F})}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 333.33~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is critically damped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical-A2" /> to calculate the coefficients.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= 0.6~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= (333.33~\textrm{Np/s})(0.6~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 200~\textrm{A/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug all of these quantities into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical" /> to derive the equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left(0.6~\textrm{A}~~\textrm{e}^{-333t} + 200~\textrm{A/s}~t~\textrm{e}^{-333t}\right)~u(t) + 0.6~\textrm{A}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>The current is plotted below as a function of time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-example-fig5">
							<shortdescription>Graph of the equation defined above.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(ms), xmin=-3, xmax=30, ylabel=$i(t)$~(A)]
										\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-3,0.6) (0,0.6)};
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:30, samples=100] {0.6*exp(-0.333*x)+0.2*x*exp(-0.333*x)};
									\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
				
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Homogeneous parallel RLC circuit with parasitic resistance</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit shown below. The switch is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds. The inductor has a parasitic resistance of 60<nbsp />&#8486;, which is not explicitly shown on the circuit diagram.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-parasitic-example-fig1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three nodes consisting of a 200mA current source, SPST switch, 100mH inductor, 22nF capacitor, and 47ohm resistor. The 200mA source connects to the SPST switch at node a. The 200mA source flows into node a. The SPST switch, inductor, capacitor, and resistor connect at node b. The source, resistor, capacitor, and inductor connect at node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node b to node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the resistor from node b to node c. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=200~mA]							(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [cute opening switch]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=100~mH, *-*, i^=$i(t)$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=22~nF, *-*]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=47~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the initial conditions of the circuit. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by its parasitic resistance.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-parasitic-example-fig2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and two nodes consisting of a 200mA source, 60ohm resistor, and 47ohm resistor. All three circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b. The source flows out of node b and into node a. Branch current i(0) flows through the 60ohm resistor from node a to node b. Voltage v(0) drops over the 47ohm resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=200~mA]									(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=60~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$i(0)$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=47~$\Omega$, v^=$v(0)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(0)</m>. This quantity is continuous with time.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= 200~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{60~\Omega//47~\Omega}{60~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 87.85~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>v(0)</m>. This quantity is also continuous with time. There will be no need to analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= (0.2~\textrm{A})(60~\Omega//47~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.27~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-ipzero" /> to calculate the initial first derivative of the current.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i'(0) \amp= \frac{5.27~\textrm{V} - (0.08785~\textrm{A})(60~\Omega)}{0.1~\textrm{H}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0~\textrm{A/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-alpha" /> to calculate the damping parameter.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{1}{2(47~\Omega)(22\times10^{-9}\textrm{F})}+\frac{60~\Omega}{2(0.1~\textrm{H})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 483858.99~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-omega0" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \sqrt{\frac{1}{(0.1~\textrm{H})(22\times10^{-9}~\textrm{F})}+\frac{60~\Omega}{(47~\Omega)(0.1~\textrm{H})(22\times10^{-9}\textrm{F})}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 32168.56~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is overdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots2" /> to calculate the roots of the characteristic equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>s_1 \amp= -483858.99 + \sqrt{483858.99^2 - 32168.56^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -1070.52</mrow>
							<mrow>s_2 \amp= -483858.99 - \sqrt{483858.99^2 - 32168.56^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -966647.46</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A1" /> <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A2" /> to calculate the coefficients of the equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= \frac{(87.85)(-966647.46)}{-966647.46+1070.52}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 87.95~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= \frac{-(87.85)(-1070.52)}{-966647.46+1070.52}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.10~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug all of these quantities into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over" /> to derive the equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left(87.95~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-1070.52t} - 0.10~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-966647.46t}\right)~u(t) + 87.85~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>The current is plotted below as a function of time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-parasitic-example-fig3">
							<shortdescription>Graph of the equation defined above.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(ms), xmin=-1, xmax=10, ylabel=$i(t)$~(mA)]
										\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-1,87.85) (0,87.85)};
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:10, samples=50] {87.95*exp(-1.07*x)-0.1*exp(-966*x)};
									\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>
				
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Homogeneous general second-order circuit in symbolic form</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Derive a second order differential equation in terms of <m>v(t)</m> (in standard form) for the circuit shown below. The switch is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-symbolic-example-fig1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a voltage source Vs, resistor Rs, SPST switch, capacitor C, resistor R1, resistor R2, and inductor L. The high-potential end of Vs and Rs connect at node a. Rs and the SPST switch connect at node b. The SPST switch, C, R1, and L connect at node c. L and R2 connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vs, C, R1, and R2 connect at node e. Voltage v(t) drops over R2 from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute opening switch]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]						(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$]				(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, v^=$v(t)$]	(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>First, analyze the initial conditions of the circuit. The inductor can be replaced by a short and the capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-symbolic-example-fig2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vs and resistors Rs, R1, and R2. The high-potential end of Vs and Rs connect at node a. Rs, R1, and R2 connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vs, R1, and R2 connect at node c. Voltage v(0) drops over R2 from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, v^=$v(0)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use a voltage divider to calculate the initial voltage. This value will be continuous with time. This is because resistor <m>R_2</m> is in series with the inductor, and the current through the inductor is continuous with time. Therefore, <m>R_2i(t)</m>, which is equal to <m>v(t)</m>, will be continuous with time. There will be no need to analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to check for discontinuities.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(0) = V_S\left(\frac{R_2//R_1}{R_S+R_2//R_1}\right)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the second-order equation. Two terms (<m>i(t)</m> and <m>v_X(t)</m>) are introduced to assist in the analysis.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-symbolic-example-fig3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of capacitor C, inductor L, and resistors R1 and R2. C, R1, and L connect at node a. L and R2 connect at node b. C, R1, and R2 connect at node c. Voltage vx(t) drops over C from node a to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node a to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over R2 from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v_X(t)$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$, i^=$i(t)$]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, v^=$v(t)$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KVL around the loop consisting of the capacitor, inductor, and <m>R_2</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-symbolic-ex-kvl">v_X(t) = L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + v(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>The inductor is in series with resistor <m>R_2</m>. This means that the current through the inductor can be calculated using Ohm's law with <m>R_2</m>. This current is equal to <m>v(t)/R_2</m>. Plug this quantity into <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-general-symbolic-ex-kvl" /> to eliminate the <m>i(t)</m> term from the equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-symbolic-vx">v_X(t) = \frac{L}{R_2}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting the capacitor, <m>R_1</m>, and the inductor.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-symbolic-kcl">\frac{v(t)}{R_2} + C\frac{dv_X(t)}{dt} + \frac{v_X(t)}{R_1} = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-general-symbolic-vx" /> into <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-general-symbolic-kcl" /> and reduce to standard form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R_2} + C\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{L}{R_2}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)\right) + \frac{1}{R_1}\left(\frac{L}{R_2}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{R_2}v(t) + \frac{CL}{R_2}\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{L}{R_1R_2}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{R_1}v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{CL}{R_2}\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \left(C + \frac{L}{R_1R_2}\right)\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{1}{R_2} + \frac{1}{R_1}\right)v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{R_2}{L} + \frac{1}{R_1C}\right)\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{1}{LC} + \frac{R_2}{R_1LC}\right)v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Derive equations for alpha and omega.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{R_2}{2L} + \frac{1}{2CR_1}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \sqrt{\frac{R_2}{R_1LC}+\frac{1}{LC}}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Derive an equation for the initial derivative of the voltage drop. (Note: this comes from <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-general-symbolic-vx" />.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>v'(0^+) = \frac{R_2}{L}\left(v_X(0^+)-v(0^+)\right)</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit can now be solved given any numerical values of <m>V_S</m>, <m>R_S</m>, <m>L</m>, <m>C</m>, <m>R_1</m> and <m>R_2</m>.</p>
				</statement>
				
			</example>

			<example xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-numerical-diffeq-example">
				<title>Homogeneous general second-order circuit in numeric form</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit shown below. The switch is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are <m>R_1 = 20~\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 30~\Omega</m>, <m>R_3 = 30~\Omega</m>, <m>L_1 = 500~\textrm{mH}</m>, and <m>L_2 = 400~\textrm{mH}</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-numeric-example-fig1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of a 10V source, 20ohm resistor, SPST switch, tow inductors (L1 and L2) and three resistors (R1, R2, and R3). The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 20ohm resistor at node a. The 20ohm resistor and SPST switch connect at node b. The SPST switch, R1, and L1 connect at node c. L1, R2, and R3 connect at node d. R3 and L2 connect at node e. The low-potential end of the 10V source, R1, R2, and L2 connect at node f. Branch current iX(t) flows through L1 from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through L2 from node e to node f. Voltage vX(t) drops over R2 from node d to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=10~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute opening switch]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L_1$, i^=$i_X(t)$]			(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]						(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [L, l_=$L_2$, i^=$i(t)$]			(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in its initial steady-state configuration Both inductors can be replaced by shorts.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-numeric-example-fig2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and five nodes consisting of a 10V source, 20ohm resistor, resistors R1, R2, and R3, and two shorts (SH1 and SH2). The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 20ohm resistor at node a. The 20ohm resistor, R1, and SH1 connect at node b. SH1, R2, and R3 connect at node c. R3 and SH2 connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 10V source, R1, R2, and SH2 connect at node e. Branch current iX(0) flows through SH1 from node b to node c. Branch current i(0) flows through SH2 from node d to node e. Voltage vX(0-) drops over R2 from node c to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=10~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i^=$i_X(0)$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, v^=$v_X(0^-)$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short, i^=$i(0)$]						(7.5,0);
									draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Transform the voltage source into a current source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-numeric-example-fig3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four meshes and two nodes consisting of a 0.5A source, 20ohm resistor, and resistors R1, R2, and R3. All five circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 0.5A source flows out of node b into node a. Branch current iX(0) flows in the short between R1 and R2. Branch current i(0) flows through R3 from node a to node b. Voltage vX(0-) drops over R2 from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=0.5~A]									(-2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]	++(right:5) to [short, i^=$i_X(0)$]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, v^=$v_X(0^-)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short, i^=$i(0)$]								(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 20~\Omega // R_1 // R_2 // R_3</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20~\Omega // 20~\Omega // 30~\Omega // 30~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(0)</m>. This value will be continuous with time.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= 0.5~\textrm{A}\left(\frac{6~\Omega}{30~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.1~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use KCL to calculate <m>i_X(0)</m>. This value will also be continuous with time. Because both <m>R_2</m> and <m>R_3</m> are 30<nbsp />&#8486; resistors, they will have equal current values in the initial steady-state condition.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i_X(0) \amp= 0.1~\textrm{A} + 0.1~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.2~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>v_X(0^-)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v_X(0^-) \amp= (0.1~\textrm{A})(30~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><m>v_X(t)</m> may not be continuous with time. Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to calculate <m>v_X(0^+)</m>. Replace <m>L_1</m> with a 0.2<nbsp />A current source. Replace <m>L_2</m> with a 0.1<nbsp />A current source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-numeric-example-fig4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of resistors R1, R2, and R3, and two current sources of 0.2A and 0.1A. R1 and the 0.2A source connect at node a. The 0.2A source flows out of node a. The 0.2A source, R2, and R3 connect at node b. The 0.2A source flows into node b. R3 and the 0.1A source connect at node c. The 0.1A source flows out of node c. R1, R2, and the 0.1A source connect at node d. The 0.1A source flows into node d. vX(0+) drops over R2 from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [I, l^=0.2~A]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, v^=$v_X(0^+)$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [I, l^=0.1~A]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>From KCL, we can see that the current through <m>R_2</m> will be equal to 0.1<nbsp />A. Therefore, <m>v_X(0^+) = 3~\textrm{V}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the second-order differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-numeric-example-fig5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of two inductors (L1 and L2) and three resistors (R1, R2, and R3). R1 and L1 connect at node a. L1, R2, and R3 connect at node b. R3 and L2 connect at node c. R1, R2, and L2 connect at node d. Branch current iX(t) flows through L1 from node a to node b. Branch current i(t) flows through L2 from node c to node d. Voltage vX(t) drops over R2 from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=$L_1$, i^=$i_X(t)$]			(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L_2$, i^=$i(t)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting <m>L_1</m>, <m>R_2</m>, and <m>R_3</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-numeric-kcl">i_X(t) = \frac{v_X(t)}{R_2} + i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the left-hand loop of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-numeric-kvl-lhs">v_X(t) = -L_1\frac{di_X(t)}{dt} - R_1i_X(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-general-numeric-kcl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-general-numeric-kvl-lhs" /> to remove <m>i_X(t)</m> terms.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>v_X(t) \amp= -L_1\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{v_X(t)}{R_2} + i(t)\right) - R_1\left(\frac{v_X(t)}{R_2} + i(t)\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -\frac{L_1}{R_2}\frac{dv_X(t)}{dt} - L_1\frac{di(t)}{dt} - \frac{R_1}{R_2}v_X(t) - R_1i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= v_X(t) + \frac{R_1}{R_2}v_X(t) + \frac{L_1}{R_2}\frac{dv_X(t)}{dt} + L_1\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_1i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-nueric-almost">\amp= \left(1 + \frac{R_1}{R_2}\right)v_X(t) + \frac{L_1}{R_2}\frac{dv_X(t)}{dt} + L_1\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_1i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the right-hand loop of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-numeric-kvl-rhs">v_X(t) = R_3i(t) + L_2\frac{di(t)}{dt}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-general-numeric-kvl-rhs" /> into <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-general-nueric-almost" /> and reduce to standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(1 + \frac{R_1}{R_2}\right)\left(R_3i(t) + L_2\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right) + \frac{L_1}{R_2}\frac{d}{dt}\left(R_3i(t) + L_2\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right) + L_1\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_1i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= R_3i(t) + L_2\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{R_1R_3}{R_2}i(t) + \frac{L_2R_1}{R_2}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{L_1R_3}{R_2}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{L_1L_2}{R_2}\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + L_1\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_1i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{L_1L_2}{R_2}\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left(L_2 + \frac{L_2R_1}{R_2} + \frac{L_1R_3}{R_2} + L_1\right)\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(R_3 + \frac{R_1R_3}{R_2} + R_1\right)i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{R_2}{L_1} + \frac{R_1}{L_1} + \frac{R_3}{L_2} + \frac{R_2}{L_2}\right)\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{R_2R_3}{L_1L_2} + \frac{R_1R_3}{L_1L_2} + \frac{R_1R_2}{L_1L_2}\right)i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>0 = \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + 250\frac{di(t)}{dt} + 10500i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-damping-parameter" /> to calculate the damping parameter and <xref ref="ch7-eq-resonant-frequency" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{250}{2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 125~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \sqrt{10500}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 102.47~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is overdamped.</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-general-numeric-kvl-rhs" /> to calculate the value of <m>i'(0^+)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i'(0^+) \amp= \frac{v_X(0^+) - R_3i(0^+)}{L_2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{3~\textrm{V} - (0.1~\textrm{A})(30~\Omega)}{0.4~\textrm{H}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0~\textrm{A/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots2" /> to calculate the roots of the characteristic equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>s_1 \amp= -125 + \sqrt{125^2 - 102.47^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -53.41</mrow>
							<mrow>s_2 \amp= -125 - \sqrt{125^2 - 102.47^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -196.59</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A2" /> to calculate the coefficients of the equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= \frac{(0.1)(-196.59)}{-196.59+53.41}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.14~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= \frac{-(0.1)(-53.41)}{-196.59+53.41}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.04~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug all of these quantities into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over" /> to obtain the equation for i(t).</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left(0.14~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-53.41t} - 0.04~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-196.59t}\right)~u(t) + 0.1~\textrm{A}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>The current is plotted below as a function of time.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-general-numeric-example-fig6">
							<shortdescription>Graph of the equation defined above.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(ms), xmin=-8, xmax=80, ylabel=$i(t)$~(mA)]
										\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-8,100) (0,100)};
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:80, samples=100] {140*exp(-0.053*x)-40*exp(-0.197*x)};
									\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
				
			</example>
	
  		</subsection>

	</section>


	<section xml:id="ch7-nonhomocircuits">
		<title>Constant-Input Non-Homogeneous Second-Order Circuits</title>

		<introduction>

			<p>A non-homogeneous second-order circuit means that <m>y(t)</m> in the second-order differential equation (described by <xref ref="ch7-secondorderlinearode-standard" />) will not be equal to zero.</p>
			
			<p>To solve a non-homogeneous second-order equation, first find the general solution, which is the process used when solving the characteristic equation to calculate the solution to a homogeneous second-order equation. Then, find the <term>particular solution</term> (<m>f_P(t)</m>). These two solutions are summed together to derive the overall circuit response. Deriving the particular solution is the only new aspect to solving a constant-input non-homogeneous second-order circuit.</p>

			<p>The solutions presented below are universally true for all homogeneous and constant-input non-homogeneous second-order circuits. Homogeneous circuits are simply a special case where <m>v_P(t) = 0</m> or <m>i_P(t) = 0</m>.</p>
			
			<p>The value of the particular solution for constant-input non-homogeneous circuits are given in <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-particular-vpt" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-particular-ipt" />.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-particular-vpt">v_P(t) \amp= v(\infty)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-particular-ipt">i_P(t) \amp= i(\infty)</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-overdamped-solution">
			<title>The Overdamped Solution</title>

			<p>The solution to a constant-input non-homogeneous overdamped circuit is described in <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over" />, with the equation coefficients <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m> defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over-A2" />, respectively. Note that this equation is defined with voltage terms. If calculating current, all <m>v(t)</m> terms can be replaced by <m>i(t)</m> terms.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over">v(t) \amp= \left(v_P(t) + A_1\textrm{e}^{s_1t} + A_2\textrm{e}^{s_2t}\right) u(t) + v(0^-)~u(-t)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over-A1">A_1 \amp= \frac{s_2(v(0^+)-v(\infty))-v'(0^+)}{s_2-s_1}</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over-A2">A_2 \amp= \frac{v'(0^+)-s_1(v(0^+)-v(\infty))}{s_2-s_1}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Coefficients <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m> will have a unit corresponding to the quantity being described by the equation. (Volts for voltage, amps or milliamps for current.) These two coefficients are derived by solving <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over" /> at <m>t=0^+</m> and by solving the first derivative of <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over" /> at <m>t=0^+</m>. This derivation is shown in <xref ref="ch7-non-homogeneous-overdamped-coefficient-derivation" />.</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-overdamped-coefficient-derivation">
				<p>Start by solving <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over" /> when <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= v_P(0^+) + A_1\textrm{e}^{s_1*0} + A_2\textrm{e}^{s_2*0}</mrow>
						<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= v(\infty) + A_1 + A_2</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Calculate the first derivative of <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over" /> and solve when <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\frac{d}{dt}v(t) \amp= \frac{d}{dt}\left(v_P(0^+) + A_1\textrm{e}^{s_1t} + A_2\textrm{e}^{s_2t}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(t) \amp= s_1A_1\textrm{e}^{s_1t} + s_2A_2\textrm{e}^{s_2t}</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= s_1A_1\textrm{e}^{s_1*0} + s_2A_2\textrm{e}^{s_2*0}</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= s_1A_1 + s_2A_2</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Use the equations for <m>v(0^+)</m> and <m>v'(0^+)</m> to solve for <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>A_1 \amp= v(0) - v(\infty) - A_2</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= (v(0) - v(\infty) - A_2)s_1 + A_2s_2</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= v(0^+)s_1 - v(\infty)s_1 - A_2s_1 + A_2s_2</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) - v(0^+)s_1 + v(\infty)s_2 \amp= A_2 (s_2-s_1)</mrow>
						<mrow>A_2 \amp= \frac{v'(0^+) - v(0^+)s_1 + v(\infty)s_2}{s_2-s_1}</mrow>
						<mrow>A_1 \amp= v(0) - v(\infty) - \frac{v'(0^+) - v(0^+)s_1 + v(\infty)s_2}{s_2-s_1}</mrow>
						<mrow>A_1(s_2-s_1) \amp= v(0^+)(s_2-s_1) - v(\infty)(s_2-s_1) - v'(0^+) + s_1v(0^+) - s_1v(\infty)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= v(0^+)s_2 - v(0^+)s_1 + v(0^+)s_1 - v(\infty)s_2 + v(\infty)s_1 - v(\infty)s_1 - v'(0^+)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= v(0^+)s_2 - v(\infty)s_2 - v'(0^+)</mrow>
						<mrow>A_1 \amp= \frac{v(0^+)s_2 - v(\infty)s_2 - v'(0^+)}{s_2-s_1}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-underdamped-solution">
			<title>The Underdamped Solution</title>

			<p>The solution to a constant-input non-homogeneous underdamped circuit is described in <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under" />, with the equation coefficients <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m> defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under-B1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under-B2" />, respectively. Note that this equation is defined with voltage terms. If calculating current, all <m>v(t)</m> terms can be replaced by <m>i(t)</m> terms.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under">v(t) \amp= \left(v_P(t) + \textrm{e}^{-\alpha t} \left[B_1\cos{\beta t} + B_2\sin{\beta t}\right]\right)u(t) + v(0^-)~u(-t)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under-B1">B_1 \amp= v(0^+)-v(\infty)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under-B2">B_2 \amp= \frac{v'(0^+)+\alpha(v(0^+)-v(\infty))}{\beta}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Coefficients <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m> will have a unit corresponding to the quantity being described by the equation. (Volts for voltage, amps or milliamps for current.) These two coefficients are derived by solving <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under" /> at <m>t=0^+</m> and by solving the first derivative of <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under" /> at <m>t=0^+</m>. This derivation is shown in <xref ref="ch7-non-homogeneous-underdamped-coefficient-derivation" />.</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-underdamped-coefficient-derivation">
				<p>Start by solving <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under" /> when <m>t=0^+</m>. This conveniently derives coefficient <m>B_1</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= v_P(0^+) + \textrm{e}^{-\alpha*0} \left[ B_1\cos{(\beta *0)} + B_2\sin{(\beta *0)} \right]</mrow>
						<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= v(\infty) + B_1</mrow>
						<mrow>B_1 \amp= v(0^+) - v(\infty)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Calculate the first derivative of <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under" /> and solve when <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\frac{d}{dt}v(t) \amp= \frac{d}{dt}\left(v_P(t) + \textrm{e}^{-\alpha t} \left[ B_1\cos{\beta t} + B_2\sin{\beta t} \right]\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(t) \amp= \textrm{e}^{-\alpha t}\left[(\cos{\beta t})(B_2\beta - \alpha B_1) - (\sin{\beta t})(\alpha B_2 + \beta B_1)\right]</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= \textrm{e}^{-\alpha *0}\left[(\cos{(\beta *0)})(B_2\beta - \alpha B_1) - (\sin{(\beta *0)})(\alpha B_2 + \beta B_1)\right]</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= B_2\beta - \alpha B_1</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Use the equations for <m>v(0^+)</m> and <m>v'(0^+)</m> to solve for <m>B_2</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= B_2\beta - \alpha B_1</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) - \alpha B_1 \amp= B_2\beta</mrow>
						<mrow>B_2 \amp= \frac{v'(0^+) + \alpha B_1}{\beta}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{v'(0^+) + \alpha (v(0^+) - v(\infty))}{\beta}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-critically-damped-solution">
			<title>The Critically Damped Solution</title>

			<p>The solution to a constant-input non-homogeneous critically damped circuit is described in <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-critical" />, with the equation coefficients <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m> defined in <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-critical-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-critical-A2" />, respectively. Note that this equation is defined with voltage terms. If calculating current, all <m>v(t)</m> terms can be replaced by <m>i(t)</m> terms.</p>
			
			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-critical">v(t) \amp= \left( v_P(t) + A_1\textrm{e}^{-\alpha t} + A_2t\textrm{e}^{-\alpha t}\right) u(t) + v(0^-)~u(-t)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-critical-A1">A_1 \amp= v(0^+)-v(\infty)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-critical-A2">A_2 \amp= v'(0^+) + \alpha(v(0^+)-v(\infty))</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Coefficient <m>A_1</m> has a unit corresponding to the quantity being described by the equation. (Volts for voltage, amps or milliamps for current.) Coefficient <m>A_2</m> has a unit corresponding to the first derivative of the quantity being described by the equation (volts per second for voltage, or amps per second or milliamps per second for current). These two coefficients are derived by solving <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-critical" /> at <m>t=0^+</m> and by solving the first derivative of <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-critical" /> at <m>t=0^+</m>. This derivation is shown in <xref ref="ch7-non-homogeneous-critical-coefficient-derivation" />.</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-critical-coefficient-derivation">
				<p>Start by solving <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-critical" /> when <m>t=0^+</m>. This conveniently derives coefficient <m>A_1</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= v_P(0^+) + A_1\textrm{e}^{-\alpha *0} + A_2t\textrm{e}^{-\alpha *0}</mrow>
						<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= v(\infty) + A_1</mrow>
						<mrow>A_1 \amp= v(0^+) - v(\infty)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Calculate the first derivative of <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-critical" /> and solve when <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\frac{d}{dt}v(t) \amp= \frac{d}{dt}\left(v_P(t) + A_1\textrm{e}^{-\alpha t} + A_2t\textrm{e}^{-\alpha t}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(t) \amp= -\alpha A_1\textrm{e}^{-\alpha t} + \textrm{e}^{-\alpha t}(A_2 - A_2\alpha t)</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= -\alpha A_1\textrm{e}^{-\alpha *0} + \textrm{e}^{-\alpha *0}(A_2 - A_2\alpha *0)</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= -\alpha A_1 + A_2</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Use the equations for <m>v(0^+)</m> and <m>v'(0^+)</m> to solve for <m>A_2</m>.</p>
				
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= -\alpha A_1 + A_2</mrow>
						<mrow>v'(0^+) + \alpha A_1 \amp= A_2</mrow>
						<mrow>A_2 \amp= v'(0^+) + \alpha (v(0^+) - v(\infty))</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>
	
  		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-examples">
			<title>Examples</title>

			<example>
				<title>Constant-input non-homogeneous general second-order circuit</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit below. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are <m>V_S = 20~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>R_S = 10~\Omega</m>, <m>R_1 = 1~\textrm{k}\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 10~\Omega</m>, <m>L = 20~\textrm{mH}</m>, and <m>C = 100~\textrm{nF}</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors (Rs, R1, and R2), inductor L, capacitor C, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node a. Rs connects to the SPST switch at node b. The SPST switch and L connect at node c. L, R1, and R2 connect at node d. R2 and C connect at node e. The low-potential end of Vs, R1, and C connect at node f. branch current i(t) flows through L from node c to node d. Voltage vX(t) drops over R1 from node d to node f. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node e to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute closing switch]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$, i^=$i(t)$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]							(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]					(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The initial parameter values are all equal to zero, as no source is connected to the circuit in the initial conditions. However, it's possible that <m>v_X(t)</m> may not be continuous with time. Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>. The inductor can be replaced by a 0<nbsp />A current source and the capacitor can be replaced by a 0<nbsp />V voltage source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors (Rs, R1, and R2), a 0V source, and a 0A source. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node a. Rs and the 0A source connect at node b. The 0A source flows out of node b. The 0A source, R1, and R2 connect at node c. The 0A source flows into node c. R2 and the high-potential end of the 0V source connect at node d. The low-potential end of both voltage sources and R1 connect at node e. vX(0+) drops over R1 from node c to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [I, l^=0~A]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$v_X(0^+)$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [V, l^=0~V]							(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 0<nbsp />A source acts as an open, and the 0<nbsp />V source acts as a short. Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two resistors R1 and R2 connected in series. vX(0+) drops over resistor R1.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, v^=$v_X(0^+)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]								(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The value of <m>v_X(0^+)</m> is equal to zero.</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition to calculate the particular solution, <m>v(\infty)</m>. The inductor can be replaced by a short and the capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a voltage source Vs and three resistors (Rs, R1, and R2). The high-potential end of Vs and Rs connect at node a. Rs, R1, and R2 connect at node b. R2 floats at node c. The low-potential end of Vs and R1 connect at node d. v(infinity) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= 20~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{1000~\Omega}{1010~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 19.802~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive a differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-figure5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors (Rs, R1, and R2), inductor L, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node a. Rs and L connect at node b. L, R1, and R2 connect at node c. R2 and C connect at node d. Vs, R1, and C connect at node e. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node b to node c. vX(t) drops over R1 from node c to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$, i^=$i(t)$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					
					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting <m>L</m>, <m>R_1</m>, and <m>R_2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-kcl">i(t) = \frac{v_X(t)}{R_1} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the left-hand loop of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-kvl-lhs">V_S = R_si(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + v_X(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-kcl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-kvl-lhs" /> to remove <m>i(t)</m> terms.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>V_S \amp= R_s\left(\frac{v_X(t)}{R_1} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}\right) + L\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{v_X(t)}{R_1} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}\right) + v_X(t)</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-almost">\amp= \frac{R_S}{R_1}v_X(t) + R_SC\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{L}{R_1}\frac{dv_X(t)}{dt} + LC\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + v_X(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the right-hand loop of the circuit. The current through <m>R_2</m> and the capacitor is defined by <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-kvl-rhs">v_X(t) = R_2C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-kvl-rhs" /> into <xref ref="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-almost" /> to remove <m>v_X(t)</m> terms. Then, reduce to standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_S \amp= \frac{R_S}{R_1}\left(R_2C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)\right) + R_SC\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{L}{R_1}\frac{d}{dt}\left(R_2C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)\right) + LC\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + R_2C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{R_SR_2C}{R_1}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{R_S}{R_1}v(t) + R_SC\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{LR_2C}{R_1}\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{L}{R_1}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + LC\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + R_2C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(\frac{LC(R_1+R_2)}{R_1}\right)\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{R_SR_2C}{R_1}+R_SC + \frac{L}{R_1} + R_2C\right)\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \left(1 + \frac{R_S}{R_1}\right)v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_SR_1}{LC(R_1+R_2)} \amp= \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{R_SR_2+R_SR_1+R_1R_2}{L(R_1+R_2)} + \frac{1}{C(R_1+R)2}\right)\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{R_1+R_S}{LC(R_1+R_2)}\right)v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug component values into the differential equation, using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>9.9\times10^9 = \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + 10896.04\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \left(5\times10^8\right)v(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-damping-parameter" /> to calculate the damping parameter and use <xref ref="ch7-eq-resonant-frequency" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{10896.04}{2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5448.02~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \sqrt{5\times10^8}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 22360.68~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is underdamped.</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-kvl-rhs" /> to calculate <m>v'(0^+)</m>. Because both <m>v(0^+)</m> and <m>v_X(0^+)</m> are zero, <m>v'(0^+)</m> will also be zero.</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-oscillation-freq" /> to calculate the oscillation frequency of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\beta \amp= \sqrt{22360.68^2 - 5448.02^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 21686.84~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under-B1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under-B2" /> to calculate the coefficients of the equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>B_1 \amp= -19.802~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>B_2 \amp= \frac{-(5448.02)(19.80)}{21686.84}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -4.97~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug all of these quantities into <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under" /> to obtain the equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left(19.8~\textrm{V} + \textrm{e}^{-5450 t} \left[ -19.8~\textrm{V}~\cos(21700 t) -4.97~\textrm{V}~\sin(21700 t) \right]\right)~u(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>The voltage is plotted below as a function of time.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-const-non-homogeneous-example1-figure6">
							<shortdescription>Graph of the equation defined above.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(ms), xmin=-0.18, xmax=1.8, ylabel=$v(t)$~(V)]
										\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-1,0) (0,0)};
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:1.8, samples=200] {19.80+exp(-5.45*x)*(-19.8*cos(deg(21.69*x))-4.97*(sin(deg(21.69*x))))};
									\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
				
			</example>
	
  		</subsection>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch7-variableinput">
		<title>Non-Constant Input Non-Homogeneous Second-Order Circuits</title>

		<introduction>

			<p>In general, the solution to the differential equation for a second-order circuit is <m>v(t) = v_G(t) + v_P(t)</m>, where <m>v_G(t)</m> is the general solution, which was introduced in <xref ref="ch7-secondorderresponses" />. <m>v_P(t)</m> is the particular solution. It was introduced in <xref ref="ch7-nonhomocircuits" /> in the context of constant-input non-homogeneous circuits.</p>

			<p>It is possible for a second-order circuit to be connected to a variable input source (rather than a constant-input source) in the final circuit configuration. In this case, the particular solution will no longer be equal to the final steady-state value of the voltage or current. The particular solution for commonly used sources is given in <xref ref="ch7-particularsolutions-table" />.</p>

			<table xml:id="ch7-particularsolutions-table">
				<title>Particular solutions for commonly used variable sources.</title>
  				<tabular halign="left" width="100%">
					<col width="15%" />
					<col width="30%" />
					<col width="30%" />
					<col width="25%" />
    				<row header="yes" bottom="medium">
      					<cell><p>Source Type</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>Equation</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>Particular Solution</p></cell>
						<cell><p>Notes</p></cell>
	    			</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>Polynomial</p></cell>
      					<cell><m>A_2t^2 + A_1t + A_0</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>K_2t^2 + K_1t + K_0</m></cell>
      					<cell><p>If 0 is not a root of the characteristic equation.</p></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell><m>K_2t^3 + K_1t^2 + K_0t</m></cell>
      					<cell><p>If 0 is a single root of the characteristic equation.</p></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="medium">
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell><m>K_2t^4 + K_1t^3 + K_0t^2</m></cell>
      					<cell><p>If 0 is a double root of the characteristic equation.</p></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>Exponential</p></cell>
      					<cell><m>A\textrm{e}^{-at}</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>K\textrm{e}^{-at}</m></cell>
      					<cell><p>If <m>a</m> is not a root of the characteristic equation.</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell><m>Kt\textrm{e}^{-at}</m></cell>
      					<cell><p>If <m>a</m> is a single root of the characteristic equation.</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="medium">
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell><m>Kt^2\textrm{e}^{-at}</m></cell>
      					<cell><p>If <m>a</m> is a double root of the characteristic equation.</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="medium">
      					<cell><p>Cosine</p></cell>
      					<cell><m>A\cos{\omega t}</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>K_1\cos{\omega t} + K_2\sin{\omega t}</m></cell>
      					<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="medium">
      					<cell><p>Sine</p></cell>
      					<cell><m>A\sin{\omega t}</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>K_1\cos{\omega t} + K_2\sin{\omega t}</m></cell>
      					<cell></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</table>

			<p>The procedure for solving these types of circuits follows.</p>
			
			<p>
				<ol>
					<li><p>Derive the differential equation corresponding to the circuit.</p></li>
					<li><p>Calculate the damping parameter, resonant frequency, and roots of the general solution.</p></li>
					<li><p>Calculate the initial conditions of the circuit (including the first derivative of the initial condition).</p></li>
					<li><p>Refer to <xref ref="ch7-particularsolutions-table" /> to find the format for the particular solution. Plug <m>v_P(t)</m> or <m>i_P(t)</m> into the differential equation to calculate the coefficients (<m>K_1</m>, <m>K_2</m>, etc.) of the particular solution.</p></li>
					<li><p>Calculate the general solution coefficients (<m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m> or <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m>) using the following two equations:</p>
						<ul>
							<li><p><m>v(0) = v_P(0) + v_G(0)</m></p></li>
							<li><p><m>v'(0) = v'_P(0) + v'_G(0)</m></p></li>
						</ul></li>
				</ol>
			</p>

			<p>This method is cumbersome and not ideal for solving this type of circuit. A better method will be introduced in <xref ref="ch11-laplace" />.</p>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch7-varinput-examples">
			<title>Examples</title>

			<example>
				<title>Polynomial input non-homogeneous second-order circuit (symbolic)</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit below.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-varinput-symbolic-example1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with current source (Is*t*u(t)), resistor R, capacitor C, and inductor L all connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The current source flows out of node b into node a. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S~t~u(t)$]			(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, i^=$i(t)$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The source does not supply any current until <m>t = 0</m>, therefore, the initial conditions will be zero.</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit when <m>t \gt 0</m> to derive a differential equation for the circuit. (Because the time is greater than zero, the step function term can be eliminated from the source.)</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-varinput-symbolic-example1-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with current source (Is*t), resistor R, capacitor C, and inductor L all connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The current source flows out of node b into node a. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S~t$]				(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, i^=$i(t)$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at either node.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>I_S t = \frac{v(t)}{R} + i(t) + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> to replace <m>v(t)</m> terms with <m>i(t)</m> terms. Reduce to standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_S t \amp= \frac{1}{R}\left(L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right) + i(t) + C\frac{d}{dt}\left(L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{L}{R}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + i(t) + LC\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{I_S t}{LC} \amp= \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{1}{RC}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{LC}i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Note that this is a parallel RLC circuit, so the equation looks very similar to <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-parallelrlc-diffeq" />. In fact, the general solution is identical for all parallel RLC circuits.</p>

					<p>The particular form of the solution will need to be derived. Consult <xref ref="ch7-particularsolutions-table" />. The source is a polynomial. We can be reasonably certain that 0 will not be a root of the general solution, as a root of zero implies that the circuit has a constant amplitude output, which is not realizable in a circuit containing any positive resistance. (Refer to <xref ref="ch9-funcpolezero" /> for more information on this topic.)</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i_P(t) = K_1t + K_2</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug the particular solution into the second-order equation to calculate the values of the coefficients <m>K_1</m> and <m>K_2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{I_S t}{LC} = \frac{d^2}{dt^2}\left[ K_1t + K_2\right] + \frac{1}{RC}\frac{d}{dt}\left[ K_1t + K_2\right] + \frac{1}{LC}\left[ K_1t + K_2\right]</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform all of the derivatives.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{I_S t}{LC} = \frac{1}{RC}\left[ K_1\right] + \frac{1}{LC}\left[ K_1t + K_2\right]</md>
					</p>

					<p>Each side of the equation can be expressed as <m>a_1 t + a_0</m>. The coefficients on each side must be equal. This is how we will derive values for <m>K_1</m> and <m>K_2</m>. First, analyze the terms on each side of the equation multiplied by <m>t</m> and simplify. (Conveniently, this provides the solution for <m>K_1</m>.)</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{I_S t}{LC} \amp= \frac{1}{LC}K_1 t</mrow>
							<mrow>K_1 \amp= I_S</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Next, analyze the terms on each side of the equation that are not multiplied by <m>t</m> and simplify.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= \frac{1}{RC}K_1 + \frac{1}{LC}K_2</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{1}{L}K_2 \amp= -\frac{1}{R}K_1</mrow>
							<mrow>K_2 \amp= -\frac{L}{R}K_1</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -\frac{L}{R}I_S</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The particular solution is now known.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i_P(t) = I_S t -\frac{L}{R}I_S</md>
					</p>

					<p>At this point, it is not possible to go any farther in the calculations due to the symbolic form of the solution. The circuit could be overdamped, underdamped, or critically damped. The general solution of the equation cannot be solved without specific component values. However, as noted, because this is a parallel RLC circuit, <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-alpha" /> will be used to calculate the damping parameter, and <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-omega0" /> will be used to calculate the resonant frequency. The initial first derivative will be calculated using <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-ipzero" />.</p>


				</statement>
				
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Sinusoidal input non-homogeneous second-order circuit (numeric)</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit below. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are <m>v_S(t) = 5~\textrm{V}~\sin{(2\pi100t)}</m>, <m>R_S = 50~\Omega</m>, <m>R = 100~\Omega</m>, <m>C = 1~\mu\textrm{F}</m>, and <m>L = 100~\textrm{mH}</m>.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch7-varinput-symbolic-example2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of voltage source vS(t), resistors (Rs and R), SPST switch, capacitor C, and inductor L. The high-potential end of vS(t) connects to Rs at node a. Rs and the SPST switch connect at node b. The SPST switch, R, and C connect at node c. C and L connect at node d. The low-potential end of vS(t), R, and L connect at node e. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node d to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over R from node c to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_S(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute closing switch]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*, v^=$v(t)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l^=$C$]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, i^=$i(t)$]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The initial current will be zero, due to the lack of connection to the source. This current will be continuous with time because it's in series with the inductor.</p>

					<p>The voltage <m>v(t)</m> may not be continuous with time. At <m>t=0^+</m>, the voltage over the capacitor will be zero, the current through the inductor will be zero, and <m>v_S(0)</m> will be zero (this is because <m>\sin(0)=0</m>). This means that <m>v(0^+)=0</m>, as there are no sources connected to <m>R</m> at that time.</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-varinput-symbolic-example2-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source vS(t), resistors (Rs and R), capacitor C, and inductor L. The high-potential end of vS(t) connects to Rs at node a. Rs, R, and C connect at node b. C and L connect at node c. The low-potential end of vS(t), R, and L connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over R from node b to node d. Current i(t) flows through L from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$v_S(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*, v^=$v(t)$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$C$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, i^=$i(t)$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting <m>R_S</m>, <m>R</m>, and <m>C</m> and simplify.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>\frac{v_S(t) - v(t)}{R_S} \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R} + i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{v_S(t)}{R_S} \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R_S} + \frac{v(t)}{R} + i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-varsine-example-kcl">\amp= \left(\frac{1}{R_S}+\frac{1}{R}\right)v(t) + i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the right-hand loop of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-varsine-example-kvl">v(t) = \frac{1}{C}\int i(t) \,dt + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-varsine-example-kvl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-varsine-example-kcl" /> to eliminate all <m>v(t)</m> terms.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{v_S(t)}{R_S} \amp= \left(\frac{1}{R_S}+\frac{1}{R}\right)\left(\frac{1}{C}\int i(t) \,dt + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right) + i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(\frac{1}{CR_S}+\frac{1}{CR}\right)\int i(t) \,dt + \left(\frac{L}{R_S}+\frac{L}{R}\right)\frac{di(t)}{dt} + i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Take the first derivative of both sides of the equation to eliminate the integral. Then, reduce to standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{1}{R_S}\frac{dv_S(t)}{dt} \amp= \left(\frac{1}{CR_S}+\frac{1}{CR}\right) i(t) + \left(\frac{L}{R_S}+\frac{L}{R}\right) \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{di(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{R}{L(R+R_S)}\frac{dv_S(t)}{dt} \amp= \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{RR_S}{L(R+R_S)}\right)\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{1}{LC}\right)i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the left-hand side of the differential equation. Plug in the known function for <m>v_S(t)</m> and take the derivative as required. The unit is removed from the equation for ease of reading. Base units will be used from this point out.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{R}{L(R+R_S)}\frac{d}{dt}\left(5~\sin{(2\pi100t)}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{R}{L(R+R_S)}\left(2\pi 500\cos{(2\pi100t)}\right)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The differential equation is as shown below.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{R}{L(R+R_S)}\left(2\pi 500\cos{(2\pi100t)}\right) = \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{RR_S}{L(R+R_S)}\right)\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{1}{LC}\right)i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>6666.67\pi\cos(2\pi100t) = \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + 333.33\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(1\times10^7\right)i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-damping-parameter" /> to calculate the damping parameter of the circuit and use <xref ref="ch7-eq-resonant-frequency" /> to calculate the resonant frequency of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{333.33}{2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 166.67~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \sqrt{1\times10^7}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3162.28~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is underdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-oscillation-freq" /> to calculate the oscillation frequency of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\beta \amp= \sqrt{3162.28^2 - 166.67^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3157.88~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>It's now time to derive the particular solution. Because the input is a sine wave, the particular solution will take the form given below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i_P(t) = K_1\cos(2\pi100t) + K_2\sin(2\pi100t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug the particular solution into the differential equation. This process will allow us to calculate <m>K_1</m> and <m>K_2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>6666.67\pi\cos(2\pi100t) \amp= \frac{d^2}{dt^2}\left[ K_1\cos(2\pi100t) + K_2\sin(2\pi100t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp + 333.33\frac{d}{dt}\left[ K_1\cos(2\pi100t) + K_2\sin(2\pi100t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp + \left(1\times10^7\right)\left[ K_1\cos(2\pi100t) + K_2\sin(2\pi100t)\right]</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Differentiate the equation as needed to obtain a function containing entirely sines and cosines without derivatives. Each sine and cosine will be represented as having an argument of <m>\omega t</m> to keep the equations from getting too unwieldy.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>6666.67\pi\cos(\omega t) \amp= \frac{d}{dt}\left[-\omega K_1 \sin(\omega t) + \omega K_2 \cos(\omega t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp + 333.33\left[-\omega K_1 \sin(\omega t) + \omega K_2 \cos(\omega t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp + \left(1\times10^7\right)\left[K_1\cos(\omega t) + K_2\sin(\omega t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>6666.67\pi\cos(\omega t) \amp= \left[-\omega^2 K_1 \cos(\omega t) - \omega^2 K_2 \sin(\omega t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp + 333.33\left[-\omega K_1 \sin(\omega t) + \omega K_2 \cos(\omega t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp + \left(1\times10^7\right)\left[K_1\cos(\omega t) + K_2\sin(\omega t)\right]</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Collect all terms with cosines from both sides of the equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>6666.67\pi\cos(\omega t) \amp= -\omega^2 K_1 \cos(\omega t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp + 333.33\omega K_2 \cos(\omega t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp + \left(1\times10^7\right)K_1\cos(\omega t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>All cosine terms cancel out. Replace all <m>\omega</m> values with <m>2\pi 100</m> to obtain one equation in terms of <m>K_1</m> and <m>K_2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>6666.67\pi = -40000\pi^2K_1 + 66666.67\pi K_2 + \left(1\times10^7\right)K_1</md>
					</p>

					<p>Collect all terms with sines from both sides of the equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= - \omega^2 K_2 \sin(\omega t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp -333.33\omega K_1 \sin(\omega t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp+ \left(1\times10^7\right) K_2\sin(\omega t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>All sine terms cancel out. Replace all <m>\omega</m> values with <m>2\pi 100</m> to obtain a second equation in terms of <m>K_1</m> and <m>K_2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>0 = -40000\pi^2K_2 - 66666.67\pi K_1+\left(1\times10^7\right)K_2</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>K_1</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>K_2</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								9.6052 \times 10^6 	\amp 0.2094\times 10^6	 	\amp 0.0209\times 10^6\\
								-0.2094\times 10^6 	\amp 9.6052\times 10^6		\amp 0\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 2.18\times10^{-3}\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 4.75\times10^{-5}\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This gives us the following two coefficients for the particular solution.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>K_1 \amp= 2.18\times10^{-3}~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>K_2 \amp= 4.75\times10^{-5}~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The particular solution is now known.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i_P(t) = \left(2.18\times10^{-3}\right)\cos(2\pi100t) + \left(4.75\times10^{-5}\right)\sin(2\pi100t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Because the equation is underdamped, <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under" /> can be used to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \left(2.18\times10^{-3}\right)\cos(2\pi100t) + \left(4.75\times10^{-5}\right)\sin(2\pi100t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp+ \textrm{e}^{-166.67 t} \left[B_1\cos(3157.88 t) + B_2\sin(3157.88 t)	\right]</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The last step is to calculate the values of coefficients <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m>. This is where the initial conditions <m>i(0)</m> and <m>i'(0^+)</m> are used. Both of these values are zero. First, use <m>i(0)</m> to obtain one equation in terms of <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(0) = 0 = \left(2.18\times10^{-3}\right) + B_1</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now, use <m>i'(0)</m> to obtain a second equation in terms of <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m>. (Taking the derivative of the function will not be explicitly shown below.) We can calculate <m>i'(0) = 0</m> by analyzing <xref ref="ch7-varsine-example-kvl" /> and noting that both <m>v(t)</m> and the voltage dropped over the capacitor (represented as the integral of <m>i(t)</m> in the equation) are equal to zero at <m>t=0^+</m>. Therefore, <m>i'(0^+)=0</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i'(0^+) = 0 = 3157.88B_2 - 166.67B_1 + 0.0298451</md>
					</p>

					<p>Using both equations, the two coefficients can be calculated.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>B_1 \amp= -2.18\times10^{-3}~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>B_2 \amp= -1.25\times10^{-4}~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Put everything together to express <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp=  \left[ \left(2.18\times10^{-3}\right)~\textrm{A}~\cos(2\pi100t) + \left(4.75\times10^{-5}\right)~\textrm{A}~\sin(2\pi100t) \right.</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp+ \left.\textrm{e}^{-166.67 t} \left[\left(-2.18\times10^{-3}\right)~\textrm{A}~\cos(3157.88 t) - \left(1.25\times10^{-4}\right)~\textrm{A}~\sin(3157.88 t)\right] \right]~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The current is plotted below as a function of time. Note that the fast oscillations are caused by the transient response (represented by the general solution), and the slower oscillations are caused by the steady-state response (represented by the particular solution).</p>


					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-varinput-symbolic-example2-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Graph of the equation defined above.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(ms), xmin=-6, xmax=30, ylabel=$i(t)$~(mA)]
										\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-6,0) (0,0)};
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:30, samples=300] {2.18*cos(deg(0.2*pi*x))+4.75E-2*sin(deg(0.2*pi*x))+exp(-0.16*x)*(-2.18*cos(deg(3.16*x))-0.125*sin(deg(3.16*x)))};
									\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					
				</statement>
				
			</example>
	
  		</subsection>

	</section>

	<exercises>
    	<title>Practice Problems</title>

    	<subexercises>
			<title>Series and Parallel RLC Circuits</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="series-rlc-example1-diagram" />. The switch has been closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="series-rlc-example1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-series-parallel-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-series-rlc-example1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of a 10V source, 4kohm resistor, SPST switch, 500mH inductor, 200ohm resistor, and 50uF capacitor. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 4kohm resistor at node a. The 4kohm resistor and SPST switch connect at node b. The SPST switch, 500mH inductor, and 200ohm resistor connect at node c. The 200ohm resistor and 50uF capacitor connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 10V source, 500mH inductor, and 50uF capacitor connect at node e. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=10~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute opening switch]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=500~mH, i_=$i_L(t)$, *-*]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=50~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = -50~\textrm{V/s}~t~\textrm{e}^{-200t}~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Calculate the initial conditions of the circuit. The inductor can be replaced by a short, and the capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a 10V source, 4kohm resistor, 200ohm resistor, and a short. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 4kohm resistor at node a. The 4kohm resistor, short, and 200ohm resistor connect at node b. The 200ohm resistor floats at node c. The low-potential end of the 10V source and short connect at node d. Branch current iL(0) flows through the short from node b to node d. Voltage v(0) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=10~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i_L(0)$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Because the initial voltage drop is measured in parallel with the inductor (short), it will be zero. Use Ohm's law to calculate the initial current through the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i_L(0) \amp= \frac{10~\textrm{V}}{4~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.5~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example1-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of a 500mH inductor, 200ohm resistor, and 50uF capacitor. The inductor and resistor connect at node a. The resistor and capacitor connect at node b. The inductor and capacitor connect at node c. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l_=500~mH, i_=$i_L(t)$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=50~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This is a homogeneous series RLC circuit.</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-vpzero" /> to calculate the initial first derivative of the voltage. Note that the current is negative due to the fact that it opposes the direction of the current through the capacitor, which is defined as flowing from high to low potential.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v'(0) \amp= \frac{-0.0025~\textrm{A}}{50~\times10^{-6}~\textrm{F}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -50~\textrm{V/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-alpha" /> to calculate the damping parameter.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{200~\Omega}{2(0.5~\textrm{H})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 200~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-omega0" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \frac{1}{\sqrt{(0.5~\textrm{H})(50\times10^{-6}~\textrm{F})}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{0.005}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 200~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is critically damped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical-A2" /> to calculate the coefficients.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= 0~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= -50~\textrm{V/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug these values into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = -50~\textrm{V/s}~t \textrm{e}^{-200t}~u(t)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="series-rlc-example2-diagram" />. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="series-rlc-example2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-series-parallel-ex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-series-rlc-example2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with seven nodes consisting of a 28V source, 20V source, three resistors (160ohm, 960ohm, and 480ohm), and SPDT switch, 12.5nF capacitor, and 500uH inductor. The high-potential end of the 28V source connects to the 160ohm resistor at node c. The 160ohm resistor connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. Position b of the SPDT switch and the 960ohm resistor connect at node b. The input of the SPDT switch, capacitor, and 480ohm resistor connect at node d. The 960ohm resistor, 480ohm resistor, and inductor connect at node e. The inductor and high-potential end of the 20V source connect at node f. The low-potential end of both sources and the capacitor connect at node g. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node e to node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node d to node g. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position a to position b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=28~V]											(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=160~$\Omega$]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) node[below] {a} node[cute spdt down arrow, xscale=-1, anchor=out 2] 	(switch) {};
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[left] {b} to [short] ++(up:1) to [R, l^=960~$\Omega$] ++(right:3.5) -| ($(switch.in)+(3,0)$);
									\draw[]	(switch.in) to [short, -*]	++(right:0.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(0.5,0)$) to [R, l^=480~$\Omega$, -*]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(0.5,0)$) to [C, l_=12.5~nF, v^=$v(t)$, -*]	++(down:2.92);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(3,0)$) to [L, l^=500~$\mu$H, i_=$i_L(t)$]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(5.5,0)$) to [V, l_=20~V]						++(down:2.75) |- (0,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p><md>v(t) = \left[20~\textrm{V} + \textrm{e}^{-320000t}\left(6~\textrm{V}~\cos(240000)+3.83~\textrm{V}~\sin(240000t)\right)\right]~u(t) + 26~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md></p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a 28V source, 20V source, 160ohm resistor, and 480ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 28V source connects to the 160ohm resistor at node a. The two resistors connect at node b. The 480ohm resistor connects to the high-potential end of the 20V source at node c. The low-potential end of both sources connect at node d. Branch current iL(0) flows through the 480ohm resistor from node b to node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=28~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=160~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=480~$\Omega$, i_=$i_L(0)$]	(5.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5.5,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]						(5.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the initial current through the inductor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i_L(0) \amp= \frac{28~\textrm{V} - 20~\textrm{V}}{480~\Omega+160~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{8~\textrm{V}}{640~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12.5~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law over the 160<nbsp />&#8486; resistor to calculate <m>v(0)</m>. (Note that you can also calculate this by using Ohm's law over the 480<nbsp />&#8486; resistor. Try it out if you want extra practice.)</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{28~\textrm{V} - v(0)}{0.16~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= i_L(0)</mrow>
							<mrow>28~\textrm{V} - v(0) \amp= (12.5~\textrm{mA})(0.16~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= 28~\textrm{V} - 2~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 26~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example2-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of a 20V source, two resistors (960ohm and 480ohm), a 12.5nF capacitor, and 500uH inductor. The capacitor and two resistors connect at node a. The two resistors and inductor connect at node b. The inductor connects to the high-potential end of the 20V source at node c. The capacitor and low-potential end of the 20V source connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node d. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l_=12.5~nF, v^=$v(t)$]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=480~$\Omega$, *-*]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) --++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=960~$\Omega$] ++(right:2.5) --++(down:1.25);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=500~$\mu$H, i_=$i_L(t)$]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]					++(down:2.5) |- (0,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Simplify the circuit by combining the two parallel resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example2-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a 12.5nF capacitor, 320ohm resistor, 500uH inductor, and 20V source. The capacitor and resistor connect at node a. The resistor and inductor connect at node b. The inductor and high-potential end of the 20V source connect at node c. The capacitor and low-potential end of the 20V source connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node d. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l_=12.5~nF, v^=$v(t)$]				++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=320~$\Omega$]						++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=500~$\mu$H, i_=$i_L(t)$]		++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]								++(down:2.5) |- (0,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This is a non-homogeneous series RLC circuit. We will need to derive the final steady-state value of the voltage. Draw the circuit in its final steady-state configuration. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example2-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three nodes consisting of a 320ohm resistor and 20V source. One end of the 320ohm resistor floats at node a. The 320ohm resistor and high-potential end of the 20V source connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 20V source floats at node c. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node a to node c. Branch current iL(infinity) flows through the resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$]					++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=320~$\Omega$, i_=$i_L(\infty)$]	++(right:3);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]								++(down:2.5) |- (0,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The value of <m>v(\infty)</m> is 20<nbsp />V.</p>
					
					<p>Draw the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-alpha" /> to calculate the damping parameter.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{320~\Omega}{2(500\times10^{-6}~\textrm{H})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 320000~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-omega0" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \frac{1}{\sqrt{(500\times10^{-6}~\textrm{H})(12.5\times10^{-9}~\textrm{F})}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 400000~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is underdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-oscillation-freq" /> to calculate the oscillation frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\beta \amp= \sqrt{400000^2-320000^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 240000~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-vpzero" /> to calculate the initial first derivative of the voltage drop. Note that the current is negative because the current through the inductor flows in the opposite direction as current through the capacitor (which is defined from high to low potential).</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v'(0) \amp= \frac{-0.0125~\textrm{A}}{12.5\times10^{-9}~\textrm{F}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -1000000~\textrm{V/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under-B1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under-B2" /> to calculate the coefficients of the underdamped equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>B_1 \amp= 26~\textrm{V} - 20~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>B_2 \amp= \frac{-1000000 + 320000 [26-20]}{240000}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.83~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug these values into <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[20~\textrm{V} + \textrm{e}^{-320000t}\left(6~\textrm{V}~\cos(240000)+3.83~\textrm{V}~\sin(240000t)\right)\right]~u(t) + 26~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="series-rlc-example3-diagram" />. The inductor has a parasitic resistance of 10<nbsp />&#8486;, which is not explicitly shown in the circuit diagram. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="series-rlc-example3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-series-parallel-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-series-rlc-example3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of a 28V source, SPDT switch, 160ohm resistor, 180mH inductor, and 330uF capacitor. The high-potential end of the 28V source connects to the resistor at node c. The 160ohm resistor connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. The low-potential end of the 28V source, position b of the SPDT switch, and capacitor connect at node b. The inductor and capacitor connect at node d. The input of the SPDT switch and inductor connect at node f. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node f to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node d to node b. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position a to position b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) node[cute spdt up arrow, xscale=-1, anchor=in] 	(switch) {};
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[above] {a} to [R, l_=160~$\Omega$]									++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 1)-(2.5,0)$) to [V, l_=28~V]					++(down:2.75) |- (5,0);
									\draw[]	(switch.in) to [L, l^=180~mH, i_=$i_L(t)$]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(2.5,0)$) to [C, l_=330~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above left] {b} --++(left:0.5) to [short, -*] ++(down:2.08);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p><md>v(t) = \left(\textrm{e}^{-27.8t}\left[26~\textrm{V}~\cos{127t} + 5.7~\textrm{V}~\sin{127t}\right]\right)~u(t) + 26~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md></p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Calculate the initial conditions of the circuit. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by its parasitic resistance.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of a 28V source, 160ohm resistor, and 10ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 28V source connects to the 160ohm resistor at node a. The two resistors connect at node b. The 10ohm resistor floats at node c. The low-potential end of the 28V source floats at node d. Branch current iL(0) flows through the 10ohm resistor from node b to node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=28~V]							++(down:2.5) |- (5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=160~$\Omega$]					++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$, i^=$i_L(0)$]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]					++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The initial voltage is equal to 28<nbsp />V. There is no current through the inductor in this initial steady-state configuration.</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example3-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and two nodes consisting of a 180mH inductor and 330uF capacitor. Both elements connect between nodes a and b. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node b to node a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							++(down:2.5) |- (2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=180~mH, i_=$i_L(t)$]		++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=330~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$]	++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This is a homogeneous series RLC circuit.</p>
					
					<p>By looking at <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-vpzero" />, we can see that the initial condition for the first derivative of voltage will be equal to zero.</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-alpha" /> to calculate the damping parameter. Be sure to include the parasitic resistance of the inductor in the resistance value in this calculation!</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{10~\Omega}{2(0.18~\textrm{H})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{10~\Omega}{0.36~\textrm{H}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 27.7778~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-omega0" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \frac{1}{\sqrt{(0.18~\textrm{H})(330 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{0.0077}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 129.7498~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is underdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-oscillation-freq" /> to calculate the oscillation frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\beta = \sqrt{(129.7498~\textrm{rad/s})^2 - (27.7778~\textrm{Np/s})^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 126.7415~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under-B1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under-B2" /> to calculate the coefficients.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>B_1 \amp= 26~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>B_2 \amp= \frac{0~\textrm{V/s} + (27.7778~\textrm{Np/s})(26~\textrm{V})}{126.7415~\textrm{rad/s}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.6984~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug these values into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left(\textrm{e}^{-27.8t}\left[26~\textrm{V}~\cos{127t} + 5.7~\textrm{V}~\sin{127t}\right]\right)~u(t) + 26~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>


				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="series-rlc-example4-diagram" />. The switch is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="series-rlc-example4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-series-parallel-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-series-rlc-example4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of a 20V source, 200ohm resistor, SPST switch, 150ohm resistor, 20mH inductor, and 100nF capacitor. The high-potential end of the 20V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node a. The 200ohm resistor connects to the SPST switch at node b. The SPST switch, 150ohm resistor, inductor, and capacitor connect at node c. The low-potential end of the 20V source, 150ohm resistor, inductor, and capacitor connect at node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node c to node d. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute opening switch]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=150~$\Omega$, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]								(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=20~mH, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [C, l_=100~nF, v^=$v(t)$]			(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[ 117.42~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-8610t} -17.4~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-58100t}\right]~u(t) + 100~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example4-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of a 20V source, 200ohm resistor, 150ohm resistor, and a short. The high-potential end of the 20V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node a. The 200ohm resistor, 150ohm resistor, and short connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 20V source, 150ohm resistor, and short connect at node c. Branch current i(0) flows through the short from node b to node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=150~$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]						(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(0)$, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 150<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is shorted out of the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example4-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and two nodes consisting of a 20V source connected in series with a 200ohm resistor. Branch current i(0) flows through the circuit consistent with the source direction.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(0)$, v^=$v(0)$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Because the initial voltage drop is measured over the inductor (short), it will be zero. This means that <m>i'(0)</m> will also be equal to zero. Use Ohm's law to calculate the initial current.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= \frac{20~\textrm{V}}{0.2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 100~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example4-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with a 150ohm resistor, 20mH inductor, and 100nF capacitor connected in parallel between nodes a and b. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=150~$\Omega$]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=20~mH, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=100~nF, v^=$v(t)$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This is a homogeneous parallel RLC circuit. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-alpha" /> to calculate the damping parameter.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{1}{2(150~\Omega)(100\times10^{-9}~\textrm{F})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{3\times10^{-5}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 33333.33~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-omega0" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \frac{1}{\sqrt{(0.02~\textrm{H})(100\times10^{-9}~\textrm{F})}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{4.47\times10^{-5}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 22360.68~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is overdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots2" /> to calculate the roots of the characteristic equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>s_1 \amp= -33333.33 + \sqrt{33333.33^2 - 22360.68^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -33333.33 + 24720.66</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -8612.67</mrow>
							<mrow>s_2 \amp= -33333.33 - \sqrt{33333.33^2 - 22360.68^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -33333.33 - 24720.66</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -58056.99</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A2" /> to calculate the coefficients of the equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= \frac{(100)(-58056.99)}{-58056.99-(-8612.67)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 117.42~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= \frac{- (100)(-8612.67)}{-58056.99-(-8612.67)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -17.42~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug the values into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over" /> to derive the equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[ 117~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-8610t} -17.4~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-58100t}\right]~u(t) + 100~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate an equation for <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="series-rlc-example5-diagram" />. The switch has been open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="series-rlc-example5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-series-parallel-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-series-rlc-example5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of a 25V source, SPST switch, 6.25uF capacitor, and 250mH inductor. The high-potential end of the 25V source connects to the 125ohm resistor at node a. The 125ohm resistor and SPST switch connect at node b. The SPST switch, capacitor, and inductor connect at node c. The low-potential end of the 25V source, capacitor, and inductor connect at node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node c to node d. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=25~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=125~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute closing switch]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=6.25~$\mu$F, *-*]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=250~mH, i_=$i(t)$, v^=$v(t)$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[200~\textrm{mA} + \textrm{e}^{-640t}\left(-200~\textrm{mA}~\cos(480t)-267~\textrm{mA}~\sin(480t)\right)\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>In the initial steady-state condition, the inductor and capacitor are not connected to any source. Therefore, the initial voltage and current values are zero. This means that <m>i'(0)</m> will also be equal to zero.</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example5-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of a 25V source, 125ohm resistor, 6.25uF capacitor, and 250mH inductor. The high-potential end of the 25V source connects to the resistor at node a. The resistor, capacitor, and inductor connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 25V source, inductor, and capacitor connect at node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node b to node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=25~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=125~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=6.25~$\mu$F, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=250~mH, i_=$i(t)$, v^=$v(t)$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This is a non-homogeneous parallel RLC circuit. While this might not seem obvious, consider how the circuit would look after performing a source transformation on the voltage source. This makes the parallel RLC configuration more clear.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example5-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 0.2A source, 125ohm resistor, 6.25uF capacitor, and 250mH inductor, all wired in parallel between nodes a and b. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node a to node b. The 0.2A source flows out of node b into node a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=0.2~A]								(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=125~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=6.25~$\mu$F, *-*]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=250~mH, i_=$i(t)$, v^=$v(t)$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch7-series-parallel-example5-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with a 25V source connected in series with a 125ohm resistor. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the circuit consistent with the polarity of the source.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=25~V]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=125~$\Omega$]			(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i^=$i(\infty)$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= \frac{25~\textrm{V}}{125~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.2~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-alpha" /> to calculate the damping parameter.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{1}{2(125~\Omega)(6.25\times10^{-6}~\textrm{F})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 640~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-omega0" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \frac{1}{\sqrt{(0.25~\textrm{H})(6.25\times10^{-6}~\textrm{F})}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 800~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is underdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-oscillation-freq" /> to calculate the oscillation frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\beta \amp= \sqrt{800^2-640^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 480~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under-B1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under-B2" /> to calculate the coefficients.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>B_1 \amp= 0~\textrm{A} - 0.2~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.2~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -200~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>B_2 \amp= \frac{640 [0-0.2]}{480}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.267~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -266.67~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug these values into <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under" /> to derive the equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[200~\textrm{mA} + \textrm{e}^{-640t}\left(-200~\textrm{mA}~\cos(480t)-267~\textrm{mA}~\sin(480t)\right)\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Homogeneous Second-Order Circuits</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate an equation for <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="homogeneous-second-example1-diagram" />. The switch is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="homogeneous-second-example1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-second-example1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of a 40mA source, SPST switch, three resistors (20ohm, 10ohm, and 35ohm), 5mF capacitor, and 0.1H inductor. The 40mA source, 20ohm resistor, and SPST switch connect at node a. The 40mA source flows into node a. The SPST switch, capacitor, inductor, and 35ohm resistor connect at node b. The inductor and 10ohm resistor connect at node c. The 40mA source, 20ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, 35ohm resistor, and capacitor all connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node b to node c. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=40~mA]						(0,5);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]							(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [cute opening switch]			(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [C, l_=5~mF, v^=$v(t)$, *-*]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]							(10,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [L, l_=0.1~H, i_=$i(t)$, *-]		(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, -*]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(10,5) to [R, l_=35~$\Omega$]				(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[ 50.9~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-37.9} -28.5~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-67.8}\right]~u(t) + 22.4~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-example1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 40mA source, 20ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, and 35ohm resistor, all connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 40mA source flows out of node b into node a. Voltage v(0) drops from node a to node b. Branch current i(0) flows through the 10ohm resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=40~mA]								(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$i(0)$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=35~$\Omega$]						(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{20~\Omega}+\frac{1}{10~\Omega}+\frac{1}{35~\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.6~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(0)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= 40~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{5.6~\Omega}{10~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 22.4~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>v(0)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= (0.04~\textrm{A})(5.6~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.224~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-example1-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of a 5mF capacitor, 0.1H inductor, 10ohm resistor, and 35ohm resistor. The capacitor, inductor, and 35ohm resistor connect at node a. The inductor and 10ohm resistor connect at node b. The capacitor, 10ohm resistor, and 35ohm resistor connect at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [C, l_=5~mF, v^=$v(t)$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]							(10,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [L, l_=0.1~H, i_=$i(t)$, *-]		(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, -*]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(10,5) to [R, l_=35~$\Omega$]				(10,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short]							(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This is equivalent to a parallel RLC circuit with parasitic resistance. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-alpha" /> to calculate the damping parameter. Base units are used for all quantities to ensure the result has a unit of Np/s.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{1}{2(35)(0.005)} + \frac{10}{2(0.1)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.86 + 50</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 52.86~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-omega0" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \sqrt{\frac{1}{(0.1)(0.005)}+\frac{10}{(35)(0.1)(0.005)}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \sqrt{2000+571.43}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 50.71~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is overdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots2" /> to calculate the roots of the characteristic equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>s_1 \amp= -52.86 + \sqrt{52.86^2 - 50.71^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -52.86 + 14.91</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -37.94</mrow>
							<mrow>s_2 \amp= -52.86 - \sqrt{52.86^2 - 50.71^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -52.86 - 14.91</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -67.77</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-ipzero" /> to calculate the initial condition of the first derivative.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i'(0) \amp= \frac{0.224~\textrm{V}- (0.0224~\textrm{A})(10~\Omega)}{0.1~\textrm{H}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{0.224~\textrm{V} - 0.224~\textrm{V}}{0.1~\textrm{H}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A2" /> to calculate the coefficients.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= \frac{(0.0224)(-67.77)+0}{-67.77-(-37.94)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0509~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 50.8923~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= \frac{0 - (0.0224)(-37.94)}{-67.77-(-37.94)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.0285~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -28.4923~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[ 50.9~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-37.9} -28.5~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-67.8}\right]~u(t) + 22.4~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="homogeneous-second-example2-diagram" />. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are <m>L = 5~\textrm{mH}</m>, <m>R_1 = 60~\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 40~\Omega</m>, and <m>C = 2~\mu\textrm{F}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="homogeneous-second-example2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-second-example2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of a 5V source, 100ohm resistor, SPDT switch, inductor L, capacitor C, and resistors R1 and R2. The high-potential end of the 5V source connects to the 100ohm resistor at node c. The 100ohm resistor connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. The low-potential end of the 5V source, position b of the SPDT switch, R1, and C connect at node b. The input of the SPDT switch and L connect at node d. L, R1, and R2 connect at node e. R2 and C connect at node f. Branch current iL(t) flows through L from node e to node d. Voltage vX(t) drops over R1 from node e to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node f to node b. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position a to position b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) node[cute spdt up arrow, xscale=-1, anchor=in] 	(switch) {};
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(2.5,0)$) to [L, l_=$L$, i_=$i_L(t)$, mirror]	++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]				++(0,-2.5);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[above] {a} to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$]		++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 1)-(2.5,0)$) to [V, l_=5~V]		++(down:2.75) |- (7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above left] {b} --++ (left:0.5) to [short, -*]		++(down:2.08);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(2.5,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_2$]					++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(5,0)$) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]				++(0,-2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \textrm{e}^{-4900t}\left[1.88~\textrm{V}~\cos(6000 t)+1.53~\textrm{V}~\sin(6000t)\right]~u(t) + 1.88~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-example2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a 5V source, 100ohm resistor, and resistors R1 and R2. The high-potential end of the 5V source connects to the 100ohm resistor at node a. The 100ohm resistor, R1, and R2 connect at node b. R2 floats at node c. The low-potential end of the 5V source and R1 connect at node d. Branch current iL(0) flows through the 100ohm resistor from node b to node a. Voltage vX(0) drops over R1 from node b to node d. Voltage v(0) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, i_=$i_L(0)$]	(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$v_X(0)$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i_L(0)</m>. Note the negative sign, which is due to the current direction opposing the source voltage.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i_L(0) \amp= \frac{-5~\textrm{V}}{0.16~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -31.25~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v(0)</m>. This value is also equal to <m>v_X(0)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= 5~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{60~\Omega}{100~\Omega+60~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.875~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-example2-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of inductor L, capacitor C, and resistors R1 and R2. L, R1, and R2 connect at node a. R2 and C connect at node b. L, R1, and C connect at node c. Branch current iL(t) flows through L from node a to node c. Voltage vX(t) drops over R1 from node a to node c. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, i_=$i_L(t)$, mirror]		++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]		++(0,-2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									++(down:2.5) --++ (right:5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]							++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]					++(0,-2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Solve for the second order differential equation using symbolic form.</p>
					
					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting <m>L</m>, <m>R_1</m>, and <m>R_2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-example2-kcl">i_L(t) + \frac{v_X(t)}{R_1} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} = 0</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-currentinductor" /> to relate the voltage and current through the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-example2-inductor">i_L(t) = \frac{1}{L} \int v_X(t) \,dt</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-example2-inductor" /> into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-example2-kcl" /> to remove <m>i_L(t)</m> terms from <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-example2-kcl" />.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{1}{L} \int v_X(t) \,dt + \frac{v_X(t)}{R_1} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Take the first derivative of this equation to remove all integrals.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-example2-kcl-updated">\frac{1}{L}v_X(t) + \frac{1}{R_1}\frac{dv_X(t)}{dt} + C\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the right loop. Note that the current through <m>R_2</m> and <m>C</m> is defined by <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" /> because both of those circuit elements are connected in series.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-homogeneous-example2-kvl">v_X(t) = R_2C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-example2-kvl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-example2-kcl-updated" />, and then reduce to standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= \frac{1}{L}\left(R_2C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)\right) + \frac{1}{R_1}\frac{d}{dt}\left(R_2C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)\right) + C\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{R_2C}{L}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{L}v(t) + \frac{R_2C}{R_1}\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{1}{R_1}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + C\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left[ \frac{R_2C}{R_1} + C\right]\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{R_2C}{L}+\frac{1}{R_1}\right]\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{L}v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{R_1R_2C+L}{LC(R_1+R_2)}\right]\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{R_1}{LC(R_1+R_2)}\right]v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>0 = \frac{d^2}{dt^2}v(t) + 9800\frac{d}{dt}v(t) + 60000000v(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate the damping parameter and resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= 4900~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= 7745.97~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is underdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-oscillation-freq" /> to calculate the oscillation frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\beta \amp= \sqrt{7745.97^2-4900^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5999.17~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-example2-kcl" /> can be used to calculate the initial first derivative of the voltage.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= i_L(0^+) + \frac{1}{R_1}v_X(0^+) + Cv'(0^+)</mrow>
							<mrow>Cv'(0^+) \amp= -i_L(0^+) - \frac{1}{R_1}v_X(0^+)</mrow>
							<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= \frac{-i_L(0^+) - \frac{1}{R_1}v_X(0^+)}{C}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Before plugging in component values, we must first verify that <m>v_X(t)</m> is continuous at <m>t=0</m>. This is because the voltage is not in parallel with a capacitor, so we cannot take for granted that the value will be 1.875<nbsp />V at <m>t=0^+</m>. Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> with the capacitor replaced by a 1.875<nbsp />V voltage source and the inductor replaced by a 31.25<nbsp />mA current source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-example2-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of two resistors (R1 and R2), a 31.25mA current source, and 1.875V voltage source. The current source, R1, and R2 connect at node a. the current source flows into node a. R2 and the high-potential end of the voltage source connect at node b. The current source, R1, and the low-potential end of the voltage source connect at node c. The current source flows out of node c. Voltage vX(0+) drops over R1 from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [I, l^=31.25~mA]							++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$v_X(0^+)$]		++(0,-2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									++(down:2.5) --++ (right:5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]							++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l^=1.875~V]							++(0,-2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KCL at the node connecting the current source, <m>R_1</m>, and <m>R_2</m> to calculate <m>v_X(0^+)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= -31.25~\textrm{mA} + \frac{v_X(0^+)}{0.06~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{v_X(0^+) - 1.875~\textrm{V}}{0.04~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -31.25~\textrm{mA} + 16.67~\textrm{k}\Omega^{-1}v_X(0^+) + 25v_X(0^+) - 46.875~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>78.125~\textrm{mA} \amp= 41.67~\textrm{k}\Omega^{-1}v_X(0^+)</mrow>
							<mrow>v_X(0^+) \amp= 1.875~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now we can calculate the initial condition of the first derivative of the voltage.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{0.03125~\textrm{A} - \frac{1}{60~\Omega}1.875~\textrm{V}}{2 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{0.03125~\textrm{A} - 0.03125~\textrm{A}}{2 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0~\textrm{V/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under-B1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under-B2" /> to calculate the coefficients of the underdamped equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>B_1 \amp= 1.875~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>B_2 \amp= \frac{4900(1.875)}{5999.17}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.531~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under" /> to derive the equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \textrm{e}^{-4900t}\left[1.88~\textrm{V}~\cos(6000 t)+1.53~\textrm{V}~\sin(6000t)\right]~u(t) + 1.88~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive a second-order differential equation in standard form in terms of <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="homogeneous-second-example3-diagram" />. The switch is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="homogeneous-second-example3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-second-example3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of current source Is, resistors (Rs, R1, R2, and R3), SPST switch, and inductors L1 and L2. Is, Rs, and the SPST switch connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The SPST switch, R1, and L1 connect at node b. L1, R2, and L2 connect at node c. L2 and R3 connect at node d. Is, Rs, R1, R2, and R3 connect at node e. Is flows out of node e. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node b to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through R3 from node d to node e. Voltage vX(t) drops over R2 from node c to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]							(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_S$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute opening switch]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, i_=$i_X(t)$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L_1$]						(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L_2$]						(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, i=$i(t)$]				(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>0 = \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{R_2}{L_1} + \frac{R_2}{L_2} + \frac{R_3}{L_2} + \frac{R_1}{L_1}\right]\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{R_1R_2}{L_1L_2} + \frac{R_1R_3}{L_1L_2} + \frac{R_2R_3}{L_1L_2}\right]i(t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-example3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of resistors R1, R2, and R3 and inductors L1 and L2. R1 and L1 connect at node a. L1, R2, and L2 connect at node b. L2 and R3 connect at node c. R1, R2, and R3 connect at node d. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node a to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through R3 from node c to node d. Voltage vX(t) drops over R2 from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, i^=$i_X(t)$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L_1$]						(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L_2$]						(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, i=$i(t)$]				(10,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short]								(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting <m>L_1</m>, <m>R_2</m>, and <m>L_2</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i_X(t) + \frac{v_X(t)}{R_2} + i(t) = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Solve for <m>i_X(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex3-kcl">i_X(t) = -\frac{v_X(t)}{R_2} - i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the left loop of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex3-leftkvl">v_X(t) = L_1 \frac{di_X(t)}{dt} + R_1i_X(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex3-kcl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex3-leftkvl" /> to remove all <m>i_X(t)</m> terms from <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex3-leftkvl" />.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>v_X(t) \amp= L_1\frac{d}{dt}\left[-\frac{v_X(t)}{R_2} - i(t)\right] + R_1\left[-\frac{v_X(t)}{R_2} - i(t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex3-newkcl">0 \amp= v_X(t) + L_1\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{L_1}{R_2}\frac{dv_X(t)}{dt} + R_1i(t) + \frac{R_1}{R_2}v_X(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the right loop of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex3-rightkvl">v_X(t) = L_2 \frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_3i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex3-rightkvl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex3-newkcl" /> to remove all <m>v_X(t)</m> terms from <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex3-newkcl" />. Then, reduce to standard form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= L_2\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_3i(t) + L_1\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{L_1}{R_2}\frac{d}{dt}\left[L_2\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_3i(t)\right] + R_1i(t) + \frac{R_1}{R_2}\left[L_2\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_3i(t)\right]</mrow><mrow>
							\amp= L_2\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_3i(t) + L_1\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{L_1L_2}{R_2}\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{R_3L_1}{R_2}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_1i(t) + \frac{R_1L_2}{R_2}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{R_3R_1}{R_2}i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{L_1L_2}{R_2}\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left[L_1 + L_2 + \frac{L_1R_3}{R_2} + \frac{R_1L_2}{R_2}\right]\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left[R_1 + \frac{R_1R_3}{R_2} + R_3\right]i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{R_2}{L_1} + \frac{R_2}{L_2} + \frac{R_3}{L_2} + \frac{R_1}{L_1}\right]\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{R_1R_2}{L_1L_2} + \frac{R_1R_3}{L_1L_2} + \frac{R_2R_3}{L_1L_2}\right]i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="homogeneous-second-example4-diagram" />. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are <m>R_D = 20~\Omega</m>, <m>R = 10~\Omega</m>, <m>C = 400~\mu\textrm{F}</m>, and <m>L = 100~\textrm{mH}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="homogeneous-second-example4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-second-example4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of a 4V source, 50ohm resistor, resistors Rd and R, inductor L, capacitor C, and an SPDT switch. The high-potential end of the 4V source connects to the 50ohm resistor at node c. The 50ohm resistor connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. Rd connects to position b of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch, C, and L connect at node d. L and R connect at node e. The low-potential end of the 4V source, Rd, C, and R connect at node f. Branch current i(t) flows through R from node e to node f. Voltage vC(t) drops over C from node d to node f. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position a to position b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) node[cute spdt up arrow, xscale=-1, anchor=in] 	(switch) {};
									\draw[]	(switch.in) to [short]	++(right:1) to [L, l^=$L$] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(1,0)$) to [C, l_=$C$, *-*, v^=$v_C(t)$]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[above] {a} to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$]		++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 1)-(2.5,0)$) to [V, l_=4~V]		++(down:2.75) |- (6,0);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above left] {b} --++ (left:0.5) to [R, l_=$R_D$, -*]		++(down:2.08);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(3.5,0)$) to [R, l_=$R$, i^=$i(t)$]	++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \textrm{e}^{-112.5 t} \left[ 66.7~\textrm{mA}~\cos{158 t} + 47.6~\textrm{mA}~\sin{158 t} \right]u(t) + 66.7~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in its initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-example4-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of a 4V source, 50ohm resistor, and resistor R. The high-potential end of the 4V source connects to the 50ohm resistor at node a. The 50ohm resistor and R connect at node b. R and the low-potential end of the 4V source connect at node c. Branch current i(0) flows through R from node b to node c. Voltage vC(0) drops over R from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=4~V]								++(down:2.5) |- (2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$]					++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, v^=$v_C(0)$, i_=$i(0)$]	++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(0)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= \frac{4~\textrm{V}}{50~\Omega + 10~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{4~\textrm{V}}{60~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0667~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 66.6667~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v_C(0)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v_C(0) \amp= 4~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{10~\Omega}{10~\Omega+50~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.6667~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-example4-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of resistors Rd and R, inductor L, and capacitor C. Rd, C, and L connect at node a. L and R connect at node b. Rd, C, and R connect at node c. Branch current i(t) flows through R from node b to node c. Voltage vC(t) drops over C from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_D$]							++(down:2.5) --++ (right:5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]	++(right:2.5) to [L, l^=$L$]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, *-*, v^=$v_C(t)$]			++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, i^=$i(t)$]					++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KCL at the node connecting <m>R_D</m>, <m>C</m>, and <m>L</m>. Use Ohm's law to derive the current through resistor <m>R_D</m>, and <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" /> to derive the current through the capacitor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex4-kcl">\frac{v_C(t)}{R_D} + C\frac{dv_C(t)}{dt} + i(t) = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use KVL around the right-hand loop of the circuit. Use <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> to derive the voltage dropped over the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex4-kvl">v_C(t) = L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + Ri(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex4-kvl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex4-kcl" /> to obtain a differential equation in terms of <m>i(t)</m>. Then, reduce to standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= \frac{1}{R_D}\left(L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + Ri(t)\right) + C\frac{d}{dt}\left(L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + Ri(t)\right) + i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{L}{R_D}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{R}{R_D}i(t) + LC\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + RC\frac{di(t)}{dt} + i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= LC\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{L}{R_D} + RC\right)\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{R}{R_D}+1\right)i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{1}{R_DC}+\frac{R}{L}\right)\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{R}{LCR_D}+\frac{1}{LC}\right)i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in parameter values using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>0 = \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + 225\frac{di(t)}{dt} + 37500</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate the values of the damping parameter and resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= 112.5~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= 193.6492~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is underdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-oscillation-freq" /> to calculate the oscillation frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\beta \amp= \sqrt{(193.6492~\textrm{rad/s})^2 - (112.5~\textrm{Np/s})^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 157.6190~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex4-kvl" /> to calculate the initial condition of the first derivative of the current.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v_C(0^+) \amp= Li'(0^+) + Ri(0^+)</mrow>
							<mrow>Li'(0^+) \amp= Ri(0^+) - v_C(0^+)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(0^+) \amp= \frac{Ri(0^+) - v_C(0^+)}{L}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{(10~\Omega)(0.0667~\textrm{A}) - 0.6667~\textrm{V}}{0.1~\textrm{H}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{0.6667~\textrm{V} - 0.6667~\textrm{V}}{0.1~\textrm{H}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0~\textrm{A/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under-B1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under-B2" /> to calculate the coefficients.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>B_1 \amp= 0.0667~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 66.6667~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>B_2 \amp= \frac{0~\textrm{A/s} + (112.5~\textrm{Np/s})(0.0667~\textrm{A})}{157.6190~\textrm{rad/s}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0476~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 47.5831~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug the values into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-under" /> to obtain an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \textrm{e}^{-112.5 t} \left[ 66.7~\textrm{mA}~\cos{158 t} + 47.6~\textrm{mA}~\sin{158 t} \right]u(t) + 66.7~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="homogeneous-second-example5-diagram" />. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are <m>R_D = 180~\Omega</m>, <m>R = 380~\Omega</m>, <m>C = 12~\mu\textrm{F}</m>, and <m>L = 22~\textrm{mH}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="homogeneous-second-example5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-second-example5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of an 8.1V source, 2.7kohm resistor, resistors Rd and R, SPDT switch, inductor L, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of the 8.1V source connects to the 2.7kohm resistor at node c. The 2.7kohm resistor connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. Rd and position b of the SPDT switch connect at node b. The input of the SPDT switch, L, and R connect at node d. R and C connect at node e. The low-potential end of the 8.1V source, Rd, L, and C connect at node f. Voltage v(t) drops from node d to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node d to node f. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position a to position b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) node[cute spdt up arrow, xscale=-1, anchor=in] 	(switch) {};
									\draw[]	(switch.in) to [short]	++(right:1) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(1,0)$) to [L, l_=$L$, *-*, i^=$i(t)$]	++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[above] {a} to [R, l_=2.7~k$\Omega$]		++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 1)-(2.5,0)$) to [V, l_=8.1~V]		++(down:5.25) |- (6,0);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above left] {b} --++ (left:0.5) to [R, l_=$R_D$, -*]		++(down:4.58);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(3.5,0)$) to [R, l_=$R$]	++(down:2.5) to [C, l_=$C$]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(2.25,0)$) to [open, v_=$v(t)$]	++(down:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left(-0.04~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-216t} + 3.04~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-5530t}\right)~u(t) + 3~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in its initial steady-state configuration. The inductor can be replaced by a short. The capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-example5-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of an 8.1V source, 2.7kohm resistor, resistor R, and a short. The high-potential end of the 8.1V source and 2.7kohm resistor connect at node a. The 2.7kohm resistor, R, and the short connect at node b. R floats at node c. The low-potential end of the 8.1V source and short connect at node d. Branch current i(0) flows through the short from node b to node d. Voltage v(0-) drops from node b to node d. Voltage vC(0) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=8.1~V]		++(down:5) |- (5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=2.7~k$\Omega$]		++(right:2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short, *-*, i^=$i(0)$]	++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=$R$]	++(down:2.5) to [open, v_=$v_C(0)$]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(3.75,5) to [open, v_=$v(0^-)$]	++(down:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(0)</m>. This value is continuous with time.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= \frac{8.1~\textrm{V}}{2.7~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The value of <m>v(0^-)</m> will be equal to zero, as it's measured over a short. This is also equal to the voltage dropped over the capacitor (<m>v_C(0)</m>), which is continuous with time.</p>
					
					<p>The voltage <m>v(t)</m> may not be continuous with time. Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>. Replace the inductor with a 3<nbsp />mA current source. Replace the capacitor with a 0<nbsp />V voltage source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-example5-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of resistors R and Rd, a 3mA source, and a 0V source. Rd, the 3mA source, and R connect at node a. The 3mA source flows out of node a. R and the high-potential end of the 0V source connect at node b. Rd, the 3mA source, and the low-potential end of the 0V source connect at node c. The 3mA source flows into node c. Voltage v(0+) drops from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l_=$R_D$]		++(down:5) --++ (right:5);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]		++(right:5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [I, l_=3~mA, *-*]	++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=$R$]	++(down:2.5) to [V, l_=0~V]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(3.75,5) to [open, v_=$v(0^+)$]	++(down:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 0<nbsp />V source can be replaced by a short to simplify the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-example5-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with resistors Rd, R, and 3mA current source all connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 3mA source flows out of node a into node b. Voltage v(0-) drops over R from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_D$]		++(down:2.5) --++ (right:5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]		++(right:5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [I, l_=3~mA, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, v^=$v(0^+)$]	++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= R_D//R</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 123~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>v(0^+)</m>. Note the negative sign, due to the fact that the current source opposes the polarity of <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= (-0.003~\textrm{A})(123~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.3695~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-example5-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of resistors Rd and R, inductor L, and capacitor C. Rd, L, and R connect at node a. R and C connect at node b. Rd, L, and C connect at node c. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node a to node c. Voltage v(t) drops from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l_=$R_D$]		++(down:5) --++ (right:5);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]	++(right:5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=$L$, *-*, i^=$i(t)$]	++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=$R$]	++(down:2.5) to [C, l_=$C$]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(3.75,5) to [open, v_=$v(t)$]	++(down:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting <m>R_D</m>, <m>L</m>, and <m>R</m>. The current through the branch containing <m>R</m> and <m>C</m> is denoted as <m>i_C(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{v(t)}{R_D} + i(t) + i_C(t) = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Solve this equation for <m>i_C(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex5-kcl">i_C(t) = -\frac{v(t)}{R_D} - i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>The branch containing the resistor and capacitor has a voltage drop of <m>v(t)</m>. Relate <m>v(t)</m> to the voltage drop over both circuit elements using KVL.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = Ri_C(t) + \frac{1}{C} \int i_C(t) \,dt</md>
					</p>

					<p>Take the derivative of this equation to eliminate any integrals.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex5-kvl">\frac{dv(t)}{dt} = R\frac{di_C(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{C}i_C(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex5-kcl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex5-kvl" /> to eliminate all <m>i_C(t)</m> terms.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>\frac{dv(t)}{dt} \amp= R\frac{d}{dt}\left[-\frac{v(t)}{R_D} - i(t)\right] + \frac{1}{C}\left[-\frac{v(t)}{R_D} - i(t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-homogeneous-ex5-almost">0 \amp= \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{R}{R_D}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + R\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{CR_D}v(t) + \frac{1}{C}i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> to relate <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m>. Plug <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> into <xref ref="ch7-homogeneous-ex5-almost" />, then reduce into standard form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= \frac{d}{dt}\left[L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right] + \frac{R}{R_D}\frac{d}{dt}\left[L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right] + R\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{CR_D}\left[L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right] + \frac{1}{C}i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= L\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{LR}{R_D}\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + R\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{L}{CR_D}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{C}i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left[L+\frac{LR}{R_D}\right]\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left[R+\frac{L}{CR_D}\right]\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{1}{C}\right]i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{RR_D}{L(R_D+R)} + \frac{1}{C(R_D+R)}\right]\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{R_D}{LC(R_D+R)}\right]i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values, using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>0 = \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + 5744\frac{di(t)}{dt} +1196200i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate the damping parameter and resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= 2872~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= 1093.7~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is overdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots2" /> to calculate the roots of the characteristic equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>s_1 \amp= -2872~\textrm{Np/s} + \sqrt{(2872~\textrm{Np/s})^2 - (1093.7~\textrm{rad/s})^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -216.4</mrow>
							<mrow>s_2 \amp= -2872~\textrm{Np/s} - \sqrt{(2872~\textrm{Np/s})^2 - (1093.7~\textrm{rad/s})^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -5528</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> to calculate the initial condition of the first derivative of the current.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i'(0^+) \amp= \frac{v(0^+)}{L}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-0.3695~\textrm{V}}{0.022~\textrm{H}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -16.79~\textrm{A/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over-A2" /> to calculate the coefficients of the equation. Use base units to ensure the coefficients each have a unit of A.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= \frac{(0.003)(-5528)-(-16.79)}{-5528 - (-216.4)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -4 \times 10^{-5}~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.04~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= \frac{-16.79 - (0.003)(-216.4)}{-5528 - (-216.4)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.04 \times 10^{-3}~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.04~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-over" /> to derive the equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left(-0.04~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-216t} + 3.04~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-5530t}\right)~u(t) + 3~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Constant-Input Non-Homogeneous Second-Order Circuits</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="non-homogeneous-second-example1-diagram" />. The switch is in position b for a very long time before moving to position a at a time of zero seconds. The component values are <m>I_S = 200~\textrm{mA}</m>, <m>R_S = 100~\Omega</m>, <m>R_1 = 20~\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 40~\Omega</m>, <m>C_1 = 5~\textrm{mF}</m>, and <m>C_2 = 20~\textrm{mF}</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="non-homogeneous-second-example1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-second-example1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of current source Is, resistors (Rs, R1, and R2), SPDT switch, and capacitors C1 and C2. Is, Rs, and position a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. Is flows into node a. Is, Rs, position b of the SPDT switch, C1, and C2 connect at node b. Is flows out of node b. The input of the SPDT switch and R1 connect at node c. R1, C1, and R2 connect at node d. R2 and C2 connect at node e. Voltage vX(t) drops over C1 from node d to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over C2 from node e to node b. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position b to position a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(4.5,2.5) node[cute spdt down arrow, xscale=-1, anchor=in] 	(switch) {};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]		++(up:2.75) |- (switch.out 1) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 1)-(2,0)$) to [R, l_=$R_S$, *-*]	++(down:2.92);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above left] {b} to [short]	++(left:0.5) to [short, -*]	++(down:2.08);
									\draw[]	(switch.in) to [R, l^=$R_1$]					++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7,2.5) to [C, l_=$C_1$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]		++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]						++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(9.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C_2$, v^=$v(t)$]			(9.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(9.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[20~\textrm{V} -20.7~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-0.273t} + 0.739~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-7.64t}\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The initial voltage drops will both be zero as the circuit is not connected to a source in its initial steady-state configuration. Both of these values are continuous with time, as they are voltages measured over capacitors.</p>

					<p>To calculate the particular solution, analyze the circuit in its final steady-state condition. Each capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of current source Is and resistors Rs, R1, and R2. Is, Rs, and R1 connect at node a. Is flows into node a. R1 and R2 connect at node b. R2 floats at node c. Is and Rs connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Voltage vX(infinity) drops from node b to node d. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]						++(up:2.5) --++ (right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_S$, *-*]				++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]					++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v_X(\infty)$]			++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]					++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Neither <m>R_1</m> nor <m>R_2</m> contribute to the circuit in this configuration. Both <m>v_X(\infty)</m> and <m>v(\infty)</m> are equal to each other. Use Ohm's law to calculate this voltage.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= (0.2~\textrm{A})(100~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example1-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of current source Is, resistors (Rs, R1, and R2) and capacitors C1 and C2. Is, Rs, and R1 connect at node a. Is flows into node a. R1, C1, and R2 connect at node b. R2 and C2 connect at node c. Is, Rs, C1, and C2 connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Voltage vX(t) drops over C1 from node b to node d. Voltage v(t) drops over C2 from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]							++(up:2.5) --++ (right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_S$, *-*]					++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]						++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C_1$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]		++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]						++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C_2$, v^=$v(t)$]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Transform the current source to a voltage source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example1-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Is*Rs, resistors Rs, R1, and R2, and capacitors C1 and C2. The high-potential end of the voltage source connects to Rs at node a. Rs and R1 connect at node b. R1, C1, and R2 connect at node c. R2 and C2 connect at node d. The low-potential end of the voltage source, C1, and C2 connect at node e. Voltage vX(t) drops over C1 from node c to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over C2 from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$I_SR_S$]						++(down:2.5) --++ (right:7.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]						++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]						++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C_1$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]		++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]						++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C_2$, v^=$v(t)$]			(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine series resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example1-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Same circuit as above with Rs and R1 depicted as a single resistor of value Rs+R1.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$I_SR_S$]						++(down:2.5) --++ (right:5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S+R_1$]					++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C_1$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]						++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C_2$, v^=$v(t)$]				(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting <m>R_S+R_1</m>, <m>C_1</m>, and <m>R_2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>\frac{I_SR_S-v_X(t)}{R_S+R_1} \amp= C_1\frac{dv_X(t)}{dt} + C_2\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex1-eq-kcl">\frac{I_SR_S}{R_S+R_1} \amp= \frac{v_X(t)}{R_S+R_1} + C_1\frac{dv_X(t)}{dt} + C_2\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the right-hand loop of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex1-eq-kvl">v_X(t) = R_2C_2\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plus <xref ref="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex1-eq-kvl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex1-eq-kcl" /> and reduce to standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{I_SR_S}{R_S+R_1} \amp= \frac{1}{R_S+R_1}\left(R_2C_2\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)\right) + C_1\frac{d}{dt}\left(R_2C_2\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + v(t)\right) + C_2\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{R_2C_2}{R_S+R_1}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{R_S+R_1}v(t) + C_1C_2R_2\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + C_1\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + C_2\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= C_1C_2R_2\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{R_2C_2}{R_S+R_1} + C_1 + C_2\right)\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{R_S+R_1}v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{I_SR_S}{C_1C_2(R_S+R_1)R_2} \amp= \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{1}{(R_S+R_1)C_1} + \frac{1}{R_2C_2} + \frac{1}{R_2C_1}\right)\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{C_1C_2(R_S+R_1)R_2}v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values, using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>41.667 = \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + 7.9167\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + 2.0833</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-damping-parameter" /> to calculate the damping parameter and use <xref ref="ch7-eq-resonant-frequency" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= 3.9583~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= 1.4434~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><xref ref="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex1-eq-kvl" /> can be used to calculate <m>v'(0^+)</m>. Because both <m>v(0^+)</m> and <m>v_X(0^+)</m> are zero, <m>v'(0^+)</m> will also be zero.</p>
					
					<p>The circuit is overdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots2" /> to calculate the roots of the characteristic equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>s_1 \amp= -3.9583 + \sqrt{3.9583^2 - 1.4434^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.2725</mrow>
							<mrow>s_2	\amp= -3.9583 - \sqrt{3.9583^2 - 1.4434^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -7.6441</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over-A2" /> to calculate the coefficients of the equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= \frac{(0-20)(-7.6441)}{-7.6441-(-0.2725)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -20.7394~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= \frac{- (0-20)(-0.2725)}{-7.6441-(-0.2725)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.7394~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug these values into <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over" /> to derive the equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[20~\textrm{V} -20.7~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-0.273t} + 0.739~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-7.64t}\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m> based on the circuit shown in <xref ref="non-homogeneous-example2-diagram" />. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds. The component values are <m>V_{S1} = 8~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>V_{S2} = 10~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>R_{S1} = 3.5~\Omega</m>, <m>R_{S2} = 4~\Omega</m>, <m>R = 0.5~\Omega</m>, <m>L = 1~\textrm{H}</m>, and <m>C = 1~\textrm{F}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="non-homogeneous-example2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with seven nodes consisting of two voltage sources (Vs1 and Vs2), SPDT switch, three resistors (Rs1, Rs2, and R), capacitor C, and inductor L. The high-potential end of Vs1 connects to Rs1 at node c. The low-potential end of Vs2 connects to Rs2 at node d. Rs1 connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. Rs2 connects to position b of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch, R, and C connect at node e. R, C, and L connect at node f. The high-potential end of Vs2, the low-potential end of Vs1, and L connect at node g. Voltage vX(t) drops over C from node e to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node f to node g. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position a to position b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(4.5,2.5) node[cute spdt up arrow, xscale=-1, anchor=in] 	(switch) {};
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[above] {a} to [R, l_=$R_{S1}$]		++(left:4.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$]	++(down:2.75) |- (7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above left] {b} to [R, l_=$R_{S2}$]	++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,2.08)$) to [V, l^=$V_{S2}$, *-]	++(up:2.08);
									\draw[]	(switch.in) --++ (right:0.5) to [C, l^=$C$, *-*, v_=$v_X(t)$]					++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(0.5,0)$) --++ (up:1.5) to [R, l^=$R$]						++(right:2.5) --++ (down:1.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.in)+(3,0)$) to [L, l_=$L$, i^=$i(t)$]		++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[-2.22~\textrm{A} + 4.22~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-3t} - 6.33~\textrm{A/s}~t~\textrm{e}^{-3t}\right]~u(t) + 2~\textrm{A}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in its initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of a voltage source Vs1 and resistors Rs1 and R. The high-potential end of Vs1 connects to Rs1 at node a. Rs1 and R connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vs1 connects to R at node c. Voltage vX(0) drops over R from node b to node c. Branch current i(0) flows through R from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_{S1}$] ++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$]	++(down:2.5) |- (5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$, v_=$v_X(0)$]						++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i^=$i(0)$]		++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(0)</m>. This value is continuous with time.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= \frac{8~\textrm{V}}{3.5~\Omega + 0.5~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v_X(0)</m>. This value is also continuous with time.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v_X(0) \amp= 8~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{0.5~\Omega}{3.5~\Omega + 0.5~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in its final steady-state configuration to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>, the value of the particular solution.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example2-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of a voltage source Vs2 and resistors Rs2 and R. The low-potential end of Vs2 connects to Rs2 at node a. Rs2 and R connect at node b. The high-potential end of Vs2 connects to R at node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through R from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [V, l^=$V_{S2}$]	++(up:2.5) to [R, l^=$R_{S2}$]	++(right:2.5);;
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$]						++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i^=$i(\infty)$]		++(down:2.5) --++ (left:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= \frac{-10~\textrm{V}}{4~\Omega + 0.5~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -2.222~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example2-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vs2, resistors Rs2 and R, capacitor C, and inductor L. The low-potential end of Vs2 connects to Rs2 at node a. Rs2, R, and C connect at node b. R, L, and C connect at node c. The high-potential end of Vs2 and L connect at node d. Voltage vX(t) drops over C from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [V, l^=$V_{S2}$]	++(up:2.5) to [R, l^=$R_{S2}$]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$C$, *-*, v_=$v_X(t)$]					++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) --++ (up:1.5) to [R, l^=$R$]				++(right:2.5) --++ (down:1.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, i^=$i(t)$]		++(down:2.5) --++ (left:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KVL around the entire circuit and solve it for <m>v_X(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>-V_{S2} \amp= R_{S2}i(t) + v_X(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-nonhomogeneous-ex2-eq-kvl">v_X(t) \amp= -V_{S2} - R_{S2}i(t) - L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting <m>R</m>, <m>C</m>, and <m>L</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-nonhomogeneous-ex2-eq-kcl">i(t) = \frac{v_X(t)}{R} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-nonhomogeneous-ex2-eq-kvl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-nonhomogeneous-ex2-eq-kcl" /> and reduce until the differential equation is in standard from.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \frac{1}{R}\left(-V_{S2} - R_{S2}i(t) - L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right) + C\frac{d}{dt}\left(-V_{S2} - R_{S2}i(t) - L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-V_{S2}}{R} - \frac{R_{S2}}{R}i(t) - \frac{L}{R}\frac{di(t)}{dt} - R_{S2}C\frac{di(t)}{dt} - LC\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{-V_{S2}}{R} \amp= LC\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{L}{R} + R_{S2}C\right)\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(1 + \frac{R_{S2}}{R}\right)i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{-V_{S2}}{RLC} \amp= \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{1}{RC} + \frac{R_{S2}}{L}\right)\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{1}{LC} + \frac{R_{S2}}{RLC}\right)i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in numerical values using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>-20 = \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + 6\frac{di(t)}{dt} + 9</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-damping-parameter" /> to calculate the damping parameter and use <xref ref="ch7-eq-resonant-frequency" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{6}{2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \sqrt{9}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is critically damped. Use <xref ref="ch7-nonhomogeneous-ex2-eq-kvl" /> to calculate <m>i'(0^+)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i'(0^+) \amp= \frac{-v_X(0^+) - V_{S2} - R_{S2}i(0^+)}{L}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-1 - 10 - (4)(2)}{1}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -19~\textrm{A/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-critical-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-critical-A2" /> to calculate the coefficients of the equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= 2~\textrm{A} + 2.22~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.22~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= -19~\textrm{A/s} + (3~\textrm{Np/s})(2~\textrm{A} + 2.22~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -6.33~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug everything into <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-critical" /> to obtain an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[-2.22~\textrm{A} + 4.22~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-3t} - 6.33~\textrm{A/s}~t~\textrm{e}^{-3t}\right]~u(t) + 2~\textrm{A}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="nonhomogeneous-example3-diagram" />. Switch <m>S_1</m> is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds. Switch <m>S_2</m> is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The component values are <m>L = 50~\textrm{mH}</m>, <m>C = 20~\mu\textrm{F}</m>, <m>R_1 = 10~\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 20~\Omega</m>, and <m>V_S = 15~\textrm{V}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="nonhomogeneous-example3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-nonhomogeneous-example3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with seven nodes consisting of two voltage sources (Vs and a 6V source), three resistors (5ohm, R1, and R2), capacitor C, inductor L, and two SPST switches (S1 and S2). The high-potential end of the 6V source connects to the 5ohm resistor at node a. The 5ohm resistor connects to S1 at node b. S1, L, and R1 connect at node c. R1, C, and R2 connect at node d. R2 and S2 connect at node e. S2 connects to the high-potential end of Vs at node f. The low-potential end of both sources, L, and C connect at node g. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node c to node g. Voltage vX(t) drops over C from node d to node g. Switch S1 is depicted as opening. Switch s2 is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=6~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute opening switch, l_=$S_1$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, i^=$i(t)$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]							(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [cute closing switch, l_=$S_2$]			(12.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(12.5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]							(12.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(12.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[0.5~\textrm{A} + 0.97~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-782t} - 0.27~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-1920t}\right]~u(t) + 1.2~\textrm{A}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in its initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a 6V source, 5ohm resistor, resistor R1, and short. The high-potential end of the 6V source connects to the 5ohm resistor at node a. The 5ohm resistor, R1, and short connect at node b. R1 floats at node c. The low-potential end of the 6V source connects to the short at node d. Branch current i(0) flows through the short from node b to node c. Voltage vX(0) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=6~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i^=$i(0)$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$		]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v_X(0)$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(0)</m>. This value is continuous with time.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= \frac{6~\textrm{V}}{5~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.2~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><m>v_X(0)</m> will be equal to zero, as it's measured over the inductor (short). This value is also continuous with time.</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit in the final steady-state condition to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>, the particular solution. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example3-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of resistors R1 and R2 and voltage source Vs. The low-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1 and R2 connect at node b. R2 and the high-potential end of Vs connect at node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through R1 from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, i^=$i(\infty)$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= \frac{15~\textrm{V}}{30~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.5~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example3-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors R1 and R2, capacitor C, and inductor L. L and R1 connect at node a. R1, C, and R2 connect at node b. R2 and the high-potential end of Vs connect at node c. L, C, and the low-potential end of Vs connect at node d. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node a to node d. Voltage vX(t) drops over C from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, i^=$i(t)$]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting <m>R_1</m>, <m>R_2</m>, and <m>C</m>. Collect like terms to simplify.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>\frac{V_S - v_X(t)}{R_2} \amp= C\frac{d}{dt}v_X(t) + i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-nonhomogeneous-ex3-kcl">\frac{V_S}{R_2} \amp= \left[\frac{1}{R_2} + C\frac{d}{dt}\right] %v_X(t) + i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the left loop of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-nonhomogeneous-ex3-kvl">v_X(t) = R_1i(t) + L\frac{d}{dt}i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-nonhomogeneous-ex3-kvl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-nonhomogeneous-ex3-kcl" /> and reduce until the differential equation is in standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{V_S}{R_2} \amp= \left[\frac{1}{R_2} + C\frac{d}{dt}\right]\left[R_1i(t) + L\frac{d}{dt}i(t)\right] v_X(t) + i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{R_1}{R_2}i(t) + \frac{L}{R_2}\frac{d}{dt}i(t) + R_1C\frac{d}{dt}i(t) + LC\frac{d^2}{dt^2}i(t) + i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= LC\frac{d^2}{dt^2}i(t) + \left[R_1C + \frac{L}{R_2}\right]\frac{d}{dt}i(t) + \left[\frac{R_1}{R_2} + 1\right]i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_S}{R_2LC} \amp= \frac{d^2}{dt^2}i(t) + \left[\frac{R_1}{L} + \frac{1}{R_2C}\right]\frac{d}{dt}i(t) + \left[\frac{R_1}{R_2LC} + \frac{1}{LC}\right]i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values, using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>750000 = \frac{d^2}{dt^2}i(t) + 2700\frac{d}{dt}i(t) + 1500000i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-damping-parameter" /> to calculate the damping parameter and use <xref ref="ch7-eq-resonant-frequency" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= 1350~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= 1224.74~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is overdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots2" /> to calculate the roots of the characteristic equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>s_1 \amp= -1350 + \sqrt{1350^2 - 1224.74^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -1350 + 567.89</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -782.10</mrow>
							<mrow>s_2 \amp= -1350 - \sqrt{1350^2 - 1224.74^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -1350 - 567.89</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -1917.90</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-nonhomogeneous-ex3-kvl" /> to calculate <m>i'(0^+)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i'(0^+) \amp= \frac{v_X(0^+) - R_1i(0^+)}{L}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{0 - (10)(1.2)}{0.5}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -240~\textrm{A/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over-A2" /> to calculate the coefficients of the equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= \frac{(1.2-0.5)(-1917.90)-(-240)}{-1917.90-(-782.10)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.97~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= \frac{-240 - (1.5-0.5)(-782.10)}{-1917.90-(-782.10)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.27~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug everything into <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over" /> to obtain the equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[0.5~\textrm{A} + 0.97~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-782t} - 0.27~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-1920t}\right]~u(t) + 1.2~\textrm{A}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
					

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="nonhomogeneous-example4-diagram" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are <m>I_S = 660~\textrm{mA}</m>, <m>R_S = 33~\Omega</m>, <m>R = 15~\Omega</m>, <m>L = 1.8~\textrm{mH}</m>, and <m>C = 560~\mu\textrm{F}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="nonhomogeneous-example4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-nonhomogeneous-example4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of current source Is, resistors Rs and R, SPST switch, inductor L, and capacitor C. Is, Rs, and the SPST switch connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The SPST switch, R, and C connect at node b. R and L connect at node c. Is, Rs, L, and C connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Branch current iL(t) flows through L from node c to node d. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node b to node d. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]							(0,5);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]	++(right:2.5) to [cute closing switch]	++(right:2.5) to [short]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=$R_S$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, -*, i^=$i_L(t)$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[6.81~\textrm{V} - 6.81~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-176t} + 0.002~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-8210t}\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>In the initial steady-state configuration, there is no source connected to the circuit. Therefore, <m>i_L(0)</m> and <m>v(0)</m> will both be equal to zero. Both of these values are continuous with time.</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit in its final steady-state configuration to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>, the particular solution. The inductor can be replaced by a short and the capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example4-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with current source Is, resistors Rs and R all connected in parallel between nodes a and b. Is flows out of node b into node a. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						++(right:7.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_S$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 33~\Omega // 15~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 10.3125~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= (0.6~\textrm{A})(10.3125~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.8063~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example4-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and three nodes consisting of current source Is, resistors R and Rs, inductor L, and capacitor C. Is, Rs, R, and C connect at node a. Is flows into node a. R and L connect at node b. Is, Rs, L, and C connect at node c. Is flows out of node c. Branch current iL(t) flows through L from node b to node c. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]							(0,5);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]								++(right:7.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=$R_S$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, -*, i^=$i_L(t)$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit is mostly equivalent to a parallel RLC circuit with parasitic resistance. (Instead of measuring <m>i_L(t)</m>, we are measuring <m>v(t)</m>. However, this will not change the characteristic equation of this circuit.) We have already derived the differential equation for this circuit in <xref ref="ch7-parallel-rlc-parasitic-resistance" />. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-alpha" /> to calculate the damping parameter and use <xref ref="ch7-eq-parallelrlc-parasitic-omega0" /> to calculate the resonant frequency of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{1}{(2)(33~\Omega)(560 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})} + \frac{15~\Omega}{(2)(0.0018~\textrm{H})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4193.7~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \sqrt{\frac{1}{(0.0018~\textrm{H})(560 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})} + \frac{15~\Omega}{(33~\Omega)(0.0018~\textrm{H})(560 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1201.2~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is overdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots2" /> to calculate the roots of the characteristic equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>s_1 \amp= -4193.7 + \sqrt{4193.7^2 - 1201.2^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -175.7246</mrow>
							<mrow>s_2 \amp= -4193.7 - \sqrt{4193.7^2 - 1201.2^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -8211.7</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Because we are solving for <m>v(t)</m> instead of current, we must perform KCL at either circuit node to obtain an equation to use to solve for <m>v'(0^+)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_S \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R} + i_L(t) + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>I_S \amp= \frac{v(0^+)}{R} + i_L(0^+) + Cv'(0^+)</mrow>
							<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= \frac{I_S - \frac{v(0^+)}{R} - i_L(0^+)}{C}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{0.66~\textrm{A}}{560 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1178.6~\textrm{V/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over-A2" /> to calculate the coefficients of the equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= \frac{-8211.7(0-6.8063) - 1178.6}{-8211.7 - (-175.7246)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -6.8084~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= \frac{1178.6 - (-175.7246)(0-6.8063)}{-8211.7 - (-175.7246)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0022~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug everything into <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over" /> to obtain an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[6.81~\textrm{V} - 6.81~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-176t} + 0.002~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-8210t}\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>
					
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="nonhomogeneous-example5-diagram" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The component values are <m>V_S = 20~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>R_S = 2~\Omega</m>, <m>R_1 = 5~\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 2~\Omega</m>, <m>L = 2~\textrm{H}</m>, and <m>C = 0.1~\textrm{F}</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="nonhomogeneous-example5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-non-homogeneous-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-nonhomogeneous-example5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors Rs, R1, and R2, inductor L, capacitor C, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node a. Rs and the SPST switch connect at node b. The SPST switch, R1, and R2 connect at node c. R1 and L connect at node d. R2 and C connect at node e. The low-potential end of Vs, L, and C connect at node f. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node d to node f. Voltage vX(t) drops from node c to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]	++(right:2.5) to [cute closing switch]	++(right:2.5) to [short]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, -*, i^=$i(t)$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=$R_2$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(6.25,5) to [open, v^=$v_X(t)$]					++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[2.86~\textrm{A} + \textrm{e}^{-2.75t}\left(-2.86~\textrm{A}~\cos{1.09t} - 2.62~\textrm{A}~\sin{1.09t}\right)\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>In the initial steady-state condition, <m>i(0)</m> is equal to zero. The voltage over the capacitor is also zero. This is because there is no source connected to the circuit at this time. These two values are continuous with time. While <m>v_X(0^-)</m> is zero, that value may not be continuous. Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>. The inductor will be replaced by a 0<nbsp />A current source and the capacitor will be replaced by a 0<nbsp />V voltage source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example5-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of a 0V source, voltage source Vs, 0A source, and resistors Rs, R1, and R2. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node a. Rs, R1, and R2 connect at node b. R1 and the 0A source connect at node c. The 0A source flows out of node c. R2 and the high-potential end of the 0V source connect at node d. The low-potential end of both voltage sources and the 0A source connect at node e. The 0A source flows into node e. Voltage vX(0+) drops from node b to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]	++(right:2.5) to [short]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [I, l_=0~A]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=$R_2$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=0~V]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(3.75,5) to [open, v^=$v_X(0^+)$]			++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 0<nbsp />A current source can be replaced by an open and the 0<nbsp />V voltage source can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example5-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vs and resistors Rs, R1, and R2. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node a. Rs, R1, and R2 connect at node b. R1 floats at node c. The low-potential end of Vs and R2 connect at node d. Voltage vX(0+) drops over R2 from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]	++(right:2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, v^=$v_X(0^+)$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v_X(0^+)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v_X(0^+) \amp= 20~\textrm{V}\frac{2~\Omega}{2~\Omega + 2~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 10~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in its final steady-state configuration to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>, which is the particular solution. The inductor can be replaced by a short and the capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example5-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vs and resistors Rs, R1, and R2. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node a. Rs, R1, and R2 connect at node b. R2 floats at node c. The low-potential end of Vs and R1 connect at node d. Branch current i(infinity) flows through R1 from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]	++(right:2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, i^=$i(\infty)$]		++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= \frac{20~\textrm{V}}{2~\Omega + 5~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.8571~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-non-homogeneous-example5-figure4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors Rs, R1, and R2, inductor L, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node a. Rs, R1, and R2 connect at node b. R1 and L connect at node c. R2 and C connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vs, L, and C connect at node e. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node c to node e. Voltage vX(t) drops from node b to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]	++(right:2.5) to [short]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-]					++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, -*, i^=$i(t)$]			++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=$R_2$]			++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$]				++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(3.75,5) to [open, v^=$v_X(t)$]					++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>First, perform KCL at the node connecting <m>R_S</m>, <m>R_1</m>, and <m>R_2</m>. The current through the branch containing <m>R_2</m> and <m>C</m> will be depicted as <m>i_C(t)</m>. Solve this equation for <m>i_C(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>\frac{V_S - v_X(t)}{R_S} \amp= i(t) + i_C(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_S}{R_S} \amp= \frac{v_X(t)}{R_S} + i(t) + i_C(t)</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-5-kcl">i_C(t) \amp= \frac{V_S}{R_S} - \frac{v_X(t)}{R_S} - i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Relate <m>v_X(t)</m> to the branch containing <m>R_2</m> and <m>C</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-5-kvl1">v_X(t) = R_2i_C(t) + \frac{1}{C}\int i_C(t) \,dt</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-5-kcl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-5-kvl1" /> to remove all <m>i_C(t)</m> terms. Simplify and collect like terms.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>v_X(t) \amp= R_2\left(\frac{V_S}{R_S} - \frac{v_X(t)}{R_S} - i(t)\right) + \frac{1}{C}\int \left(\frac{V_S}{R_S} - \frac{v_X(t)}{R_S} - i(t)\right)\, dt</mrow>
							<mrow>v_X(t) \amp= \frac{R_2}{R_2}V_S - \frac{R_2}{R_S}v_X(t) - R_2i(t) + \frac{1}{CR_S}\int V_S \,dt - \frac{1}{CR_S}\int v_X(t) \,dt - \frac{1}{C}\int i(t) \,dt</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-5-almost">\frac{R_2}{R_S}V_S + \frac{1}{CR_S}\int V_S \,dt \amp= v_X(t) + \frac{R_2}{R_S} v_X(t) + \frac{1}{CR_S}\int v_X(t) \,dt + R_2i(t) + \frac{1}{C}\int i(t) \,dt</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Relate <m>v_X(t)</m> to the branch containing <m>R_1</m> and <m>L</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-5-kvl2">v_X(t) = R_1i(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-5-kvl2" /> into <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-5-almost" /> and reduce to standard form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{R_2}{R_S}V_S + \frac{1}{CR_S}\int V_S \,dt \amp= \left(R_1i(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right) + \frac{R_2}{R_S} \left(R_1i(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right) + \frac{1}{CR_S}\int \left(R_1i(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\right) \,dt + R_2i(t) + \frac{1}{C}\int i(t) \,dt</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= R_1i(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{R_2R_1}{R_s}i(t) + \frac{R_2L}{R_S}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{R_1}{CR_S}\int i(t) \,dt + \frac{L}{CR_S}i(t) + R_2i(t) + \frac{1}{C}\int i(t) \,dt</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{R_2}{R_S}\frac{dV_S}{dt} + \frac{1}{CR_S}V_S \amp= R_1\frac{di(t)}{dt} + L\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{R_2R_1}{R_s}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{R_2L}{R_S}\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{R_1}{CR_S} i(t) + \frac{L}{CR_S}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_2\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{C}i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_S}{CR_S} \amp= \left[L + \frac{R_2L}{R_S}\right]\frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left[R_1 + \frac{R_1R_2}{R_S} + \frac{L}{CR_S} + R_2\right]\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{R_1}{CR_S}+\frac{1}{C}\right]i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_S}{CL(R_S+R_2)} \amp= \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \left[\frac{R_1R_S + R_1R_2 + R_2R_S}{L(R_S+R_2)} + \frac{1}{C(R_S+R_2)}\right]\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{R_1+R_S}{LC(R_S+R_2)}\right]i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values, using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>2.5 = \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + 5.5\frac{di(t)}{dt} + 8.75</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-damping-parameter" /> to calculate the damping parameter and use <xref ref="ch7-eq-resonant-frequency" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= 2.75~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= 2.958~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is underdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-oscillation-freq" /> to calculate the oscillation frequency of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\beta \amp= \sqrt{2.958^2 - 2.75^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.0897~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
						
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-5-kvl2" /> to calculate <m>i'(0^+)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i'(0^+) \amp= \frac{v_X(0^+) - R_1i(0^+)}{L}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{10 - 0}{2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{A/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under-B1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under-B2" /> to calculate the coefficients of the equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>B_1 \amp= -2.8571~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>B_2 \amp= \frac{5 + 2.75(-2.8571)}{1.0897}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -2.6219~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug everything into <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-under" /> to obtain the equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[2.86~\textrm{A} + \textrm{e}^{-2.75t}\left(-2.86~\textrm{A}~\cos{1.09t} - 2.62~\textrm{A}~\sin{1.09t}\right)\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>


				</solution>

				
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title><q>Challenging</q> Second-Order Circuits</title>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-challenge-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="challenge-second-example5-diagram" />. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds. The component values are <m>V_S = 10~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>R_S = 4~\Omega</m>, <m>R = 2~\Omega</m>, <m>r_m = 0.5~\textrm{V/A}</m>, <m>L = 5~\textrm{mH}</m>, and <m>C = 200~\mu\textrm{F}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="challenge-second-example5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-challenge-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch7-challenge-second-example5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of voltage source Vs, CCVS rm*iX(t), resistors Rs and R, capacitor C, inductor L, and SPDT switch. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node c. Rs connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. Position b of the SPDT switch connects to L and the high-potential end of the CCVS at node b. The input of the SPDT switch and C connect at node d. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R at node e. The low-potential end of Vs, C, L, and R connect at node f. Voltage vC(t) drops over C from node d to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node b to node f. Branch current iX(t) flows through R from node e to node f. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position a to position b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) node[cute spdt up arrow, rotate=90, anchor=in] 	(switch) {};
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[above] {a} to [R, l_=$R_S$]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]	++(down:3) |- (7,0);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {b} to [short]	++(right:2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, *-*, i^=$i(t)$]	++(down:3.22);
									\draw[]	(switch.in) to [C, l_=$C$, -*, v^=$v_C(t)$]					++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)+(2.5,0)$) to [cV, l^=$r_mi_X(t)$]		++(right:2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, i^=$i_X(t)$]	++(down:3) |- (7,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 2000~\textrm{A/s}~t~\textrm{e}^{-1000t}~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>In the initial steady-state, the inductor, resistor <m>R</m>, and CCVS will not be connected to a source. This means that <m>i(0)</m> is equal to zero. That value is continuous with time. The capacitor will be an open connected in parallel to <m>V_S</m>. This means that <m>v_C(0) = V_S</m>. This value is also continuous with time.</p>

					<p>In the final steady-state, none of the circuit elements will be connected to a source. This means that the particular solution is equal to zero and the circuit is homogeneous.</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the differential equation.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-challenge-example5-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of capacitor C, inductor L, resistor R, and CCVS of value rm*iX(t). C, L, and the high-potential end of the CCVS connect at node a. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R at node b. C, L, and R connect at node c. Voltage vC(t) drops over C from node a to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node a to node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]	++(right:2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, *-*, i^=$i(t)$]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v_C(t)$]					++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cV, l^=$r_mi_X(t)$]		++(right:2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, i^=$i_X(t)$]	++(down:2.5) --++ (left:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting <m>C</m>, <m>L</m>, and the CCVS.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-challenge5-eq-kcl">C\frac{dv_C(t)}{dt} + i(t) + i_X(t) = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the right-hand loop of the circuit and solve for <m>i_X(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>v_C(t) \amp= r_mi_X(t) + Ri_X(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (r_m+R)i_X(t)</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-challenge5-eq-kvl">i_X(t) \amp= \frac{v_C(t)}{r_m+R}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-challenge5-eq-kvl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-challenge5-eq-kcl" /> to remove <m>i_X(t)</m> terms.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-challenge5-eq-almost">C\frac{dv_C(t)}{dt} + i(t) + \frac{v_C(t)}{r_m+R}</md>
					</p>

					<p>One last equation that relates the voltage dropped over the inductor to <m>i(t)</m> is given by <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />. Plug <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> into <xref ref="ch7-challenge5-eq-almost" /> and reduce until the differential equation is in standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= LC \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + i(t) + \frac{L}{r_m+R}\frac{di(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{1}{C(r_m+R)}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{LC}i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values, using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>0 = \frac{d^2i(t)}{dt^2} + 2000 \frac{di(t)}{dt} + 1 \times 10^6</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-damping-parameter" /> to calculate the damping parameter and use <xref ref="ch7-eq-resonant-frequency" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha = 1000~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 = 1000~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is critically damped.</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> to calculate <m>i'(0^+)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i'(0^+) \amp= \frac{v_C(0^+)}{L}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{10~\textrm{V}}{0.005~\textrm{H}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2000~\textrm{A/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
						
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical-A2" /> to calculate the coefficients.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= 0~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= 2000~\textrm{A/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug everything into <xref ref="ch7-eq-homogeneous-critical" /> to obtain the equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 2000~\textrm{A/s}~t~\textrm{e}^{-1000t}~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch7-challenge-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="challenge-second-example1-diagram" />. The switch is in position b for a very long time before moving to position a at a time of zero seconds. The component values are <m>V_m = 16~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>a = 2~\textrm{rad/s}</m>, <m>V_S = 8~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>R = 10~\Omega</m>, <m>R_S = 20~\Omega</m>, <m>L = 500~\textrm{mH}</m>, and <m>C = 20~\textrm{mF}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="challenge-second-example1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-challenge-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch7-challenge-second-example1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with seven nodes consisting of two voltage sources (Vs and Vm*exp(-a*t)), two resistors (R and Rs), and SPDT switch, inductor L, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of Vm*exp(-a*t) connects to R at node c. R connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node d. Rs connects to position b of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to L at node e. L and C connect at node f. The low-potential end of both voltage sources and C connect at node g. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node f to node g. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position b to position a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(4.5,2.5) node[cute spdt down arrow, xscale=-1, anchor=in] 	(switch) {};
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[above] {a} to [R, l_=$R$]	++(left:4.5) to [V, l_=$V_m~\textrm{e}^{-at}$]	++(down:2.75) |- (7,0);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above left] {b} to [R, l_=$R_S$]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$, -*]	++(down:2.08);
									\draw[]	(switch.in) to [L, l^=$L$]					++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]		++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[25~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-2t} - 17~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-10t} - 120~\textrm{V/s}~t~\textrm{e}^{-10t}\right]~u(t) + 8~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in its initial steady-state configuration to calculate the initial conditions. The inductor can be replaced by a short and the capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="30%" xml:id="ch7-challenge-example1-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with three nodes consisting of voltage source Vs and resistor Rs. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node a. Rs floats at node b. The low-potential end of Vs floats at node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_S$]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]		++(down:2.5) --++ (left:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The initial voltage is equal to 8<nbsp />V. Note that the initial value of the current through the inductor will be 0<nbsp />A, due to the fact that the circuit is open. Both of these values are continuous with time.</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m> to derive the differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch7-challenge-example1-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vm*exp(-a*t), resistor R, inductor L, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of the voltage source connects to R at node a. R and L connect at node b. L and C connect at node c. The low-potential end of the voltage source connects to c at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=$V_m~\textrm{e}^{-at}$]	++(down:2.5) --++ (right:5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$]					++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]		++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This is a series RLC circuit, so we should expect the characteristic equation to resemble what we derived in <xref ref="ch7-the-series-rlc-circuit" />. Knowing that the current through the circuit is defined by <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />, we can simply perform KVL around the entire circuit loop. Then, reduce until the differential equation is in standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_m~\textrm{e}^{-at} \amp= RC\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + L\frac{d}{dt}\left(C \frac{dv(t)}{dt}\right) + v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= RC\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + LC\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_m~\textrm{e}^{-at}}{LC} \amp= \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{R}{L}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{LC}v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in parameter values, using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>1600~\textrm{e}^{-2t} = \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + 20\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + 100v(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-damping-parameter" /> to calculate the damping parameter and use <xref ref="ch7-eq-resonant-frequency" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= 10~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= 10~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>						
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is critically damped.</p>
					
					<p>The particular solution has a format of <m>v_P(t) = K~\textrm{e}^{-at}</m>, where <m>a = 2</m>. Plug this into the differential equation and solve to calculate <m>K</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>1600~\textrm{e}^{-2t} \amp= \frac{d}{dt^2}\left(K~\textrm{e}^{-2t}\right) + 20\frac{d}{dt}\left(K~\textrm{e}^{-2t}\right) + 100\left(K~\textrm{e}^{-2t}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4K~\textrm{e}^{-2t} + 20(-2)K~\textrm{e}^{-2t} + 100K~\textrm{e}^{-2t}</mrow>
							<mrow>1600 \amp= 4K - 40K + 100K</mrow>
							<mrow>K \amp= 25~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>						
					</p>

					<p>The equation for <m>v(t)</m> has the following form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[25~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-2t} + A_1~\textrm{e}^{-10t} + A_2t~\textrm{e}^{-10t}\right]~u(t) + 8~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <m>v(0^+)</m> and <m>v'(0^+)</m> to calculate <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m>. The first derivative is defined by <xref ref="ch7-eq-seriesrlc-vpzero" />, which will be equal to zero.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0^+) = 8~\textrm{V} \amp= 25~\textrm{V} + A_1</mrow>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= -17~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>v'(t) \amp= -50~\textrm{e}^{-2t} - 10A_1~\textrm{e}^{-10t} + \textrm{e}^{-10t}(A_2-10A_2t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v'(0^+) = 0 \amp= -50 - 10A_1 + A_2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -50 - (10)(-17) + A_2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -50 + 170 + A_2</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= -120~\textrm{V/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug all of the values into the equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[25~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-2t} - 17~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-10t} - 120~\textrm{V/s}~t~\textrm{e}^{-10t}\right]~u(t) + 8~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-challenge-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="challenge-exercise2-diagram" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are <m>V_{AC}(t) = 10~\textrm{V}~\sin(2\pi40t)</m>, <m>R_S = 10~\Omega</m>, <m>R = 10~\Omega</m>, <m>L = 0.5~\textrm{H}</m>, and <m>C = 2~\textrm{mF}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="challenge-exercise2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-challenge-ex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-challenge-exercise2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of voltage source vAC(t), resistors Rs and R, SPST switch, inductor L, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of vAC(t) connects to Rs at node a. Rs and the SPST switch connect at node b. The SPST switch, R, and L connect at node c. L and C connect at node d. The low-potential end of vAC(t), R, and C connect at node e. Voltage vX(t) drops over R from node c to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$V_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute closing switch]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*, v^=$v_X(t)$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[-0.080~\textrm{V}~\sin(2\pi40t) + \textrm{e}^{-5t}\left(0.644~\textrm{V}~\sin{31.2t}\right)\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The initial value of <m>v(t)</m> will be zero due to the lack of any source in the initial steady-state condition. The initial current through the inductor will also be zero. Both of these values are continuous with time.</p>

					<p>The voltage over the resistor <m>v_X(t)</m> may not be continuous with time. At <m>t=0^+</m>, the value of the source, <m>V_{AC}(0)</m>, will be equal to zero. (This is because <m>\sin{0} = 0</m>.) Therefore, <m>v_X(0^+)</m> will also be equal to zero.</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the differential equation.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch7-challenge-example2-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source vAC(t), resistors Rs and R, inductor L, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of vAC(t) connects to Rs at node a. Rs, R, and L connect at node b. L and C connect at node c. The low-potential end of vAC(t), R, and C connect at node d. Voltage vX(t) drops over R from node b to node d. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[below left] {+} to [sV, l_=$V_{AC}(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*, v^=$v_x(t)$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L$]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting <m>R_S</m>, <m>R</m>, and <m>L</m>. Combine like terms.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>\frac{V_{AC}(t) - v_X(t)}{R_S} \amp= \frac{v_X(t)}{R} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_{AC}(t)}{R_S} \amp= \frac{v_X(t)}{R_S} + \frac{v_X(t)}{R} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-challenge2-kcl">\amp= \left(\frac{1}{R_S} + \frac{1}{R}\right)v_X(t) + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the right-hand loop of the circuit. Note that the current through the inductor is equal to <m>C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-challenge2-kvl">v_X(t) = LC\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + v(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-eq-challenge2-kvl" /> into <xref ref="ch7-eq-challenge2-kcl" /> and simplify to standard form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{V_{AC}(t)}{R_S} \amp= \left(\frac{1}{R_S} + \frac{1}{R}\right)\left(LC\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + v(t)\right)+ C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{R+R_s}{RR_S}\left(LC\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + v(t)\right)+ C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{(R+R_S)LC}{RR_S}\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{R+R_S}{RR_S}v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_{AC} R}{LC(R+R_S)} \amp= \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{RR_S}{L(R+R_S)}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{LC}v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in parameter values, using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>5000 \sin{(2\pi 40 t)} = \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + 10\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + 1000</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-damping-parameter" /> to calculate the damping parameter, and use <xref ref="ch7-eq-resonant-frequency" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= 5~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= 31.6228~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is underdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-oscillation-freq" /> to calculate the oscillation frequency of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\beta \amp= \sqrt{31.6228^2 - 5^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 31.225~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-challenge2-kcl" /> to calculate <m>v'(0^+)</m>. Because <m>v(0^+)</m>, <m>V_{AC}(0^+)</m>, and <m>v_X(0^+)</m> are all equal to zero, <m>v'(0^+)</m> will also be equal to zero.</p>

					<p>Refer to <xref ref="ch7-particularsolutions-table" /> to determine the form of the particular solution.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v_P(t) = K_1\sin(2\pi40t) + K_2\cos(2\pi40t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug the particular solution into the differential equation and combine like terms.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>5000\sin(2\pi40t) \amp= \frac{d^2}{dt^2}\left[K_1\sin(2\pi40t) + K_2\cos(2\pi40t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp+ 10 \frac{d}{dt}\left[K_1\sin(2\pi40t) + K_2\cos(2\pi40t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp+ 1000\left[K_1\sin(2\pi40t) + K_2\cos(2\pi40t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -6400\pi^2\left[K_1\sin(2\pi40t) + K_2\cos(2\pi40t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp+ 800\pi\left[K_1\cos(2\pi40t) - K_2\sin(2\pi40t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp+ 1000\left[K_1\sin(2\pi40t) + K_2\cos(2\pi40t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \sin(2\pi40t)\left[-6400\pi^2K_1 - 800\pi K_2 + 1000K_1\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp+ \cos(2\pi40t)\left[-6400\pi^2K_2 + 800\pi K_1 + 1000K_2\right]</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>All of the sine terms can be used to generate one equation relating <m>K_1</m> and <m>K_2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>5000\sin(2\pi40t) \amp= \sin(2\pi40t)\left[-6400\pi^2K_1 - 800\pi K_2 + 1000K_1\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>5000 \amp= -6400\pi^2K_1 - 800\pi K_2 + 1000K_1</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (-6400\pi^2 + 1000)K_1 + (-800\pi)K_2</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>All of the cosine terms can be used to generate a second equation relating <m>K_1</m> and <m>K_2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= \cos(2\pi40t)\left[-6400\pi^2K_2 + 800\pi K_1 + 1000K_2\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -6400\pi^2K_2 + 800\pi K_1 + 1000K_2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (800\pi)K_1 + (-6400\pi^2+1000)K_2</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>K_1</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>K_2</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								-6400\pi^2+1000 	\amp -800\pi 			\amp 5000\\
								800\pi				\amp -6400\pi^2+1000	\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp -0.0803\\
								0 	\amp 1 		\amp -0.0032\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The coefficient <m>K_2</m> can be rounded to zero. The particular form of the solution is now known.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v_P(t) = -0.080~\textrm{V}~\sin(2\pi40t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>The equation for <m>v(t)</m> has the following form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[-0.080~\textrm{V}~\sin(2\pi40t) + \textrm{e}^{-5t}\left(B_1 \cos{31.225t} + B_2\sin{31.225t}\right)\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <m>v(0^+)</m> and <m>v'(0^+)</m> to calculate <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m>. Start with <m>v(0^+)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= \left[-0.080~\textrm{V}~\sin(0) + \textrm{e}^{0}\left(B_1 \cos{0} + B_2\sin{0}\right)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>B_1 \amp= 0~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The equation for <m>v(t)</m> now has the following form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[-0.080~\textrm{V}~\sin(2\pi40t) + \textrm{e}^{-5t}\left(B_2\sin{31.225t}\right)\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Take the first derivative of the above expression, and set equal to <m>v(0^+)</m> at a time of <m>t=0^+</m> to calculate <m>B_2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{dv(t)}{dt} \amp= -5B_2\sin{31.225t} + 31.225B_2~\textrm{e}^{-5t}\cos{31.225t} - 20.1062\cos{(2\pi 40t)}</mrow>
							<mrow>v'(0^+) = 0 \amp= -5B_2\sin{0} + 31.225B_2~\textrm{e}^{0}\cos{0} - 20.1062\cos{0}</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= 31.225B_2 - 20.1062</mrow>
							<mrow>31.225B_2 \amp= 20.1062</mrow>
							<mrow>B_2 \amp= 0.6439~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The equation for <m>v(t)</m> is now known.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[-0.080~\textrm{V}~\sin(2\pi40t) + \textrm{e}^{-5t}\left(0.644~\textrm{V}~\sin{31.2t}\right)\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>A graph of <m>v(t)</m> is shown below. The high-frequency oscillations correspond to the AC voltage source (particular solution). The low-frequency oscillations correspond to the transient response (general solution).</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-challenge-example2-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Graph of the equation defined above.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[ylabel={$v(t)$~(V)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=0, xmax=1000]
										\addplot[thick, samples=600, domain=0:1000] {-0.08*sin(deg(2*3.14*40*x/1000)) + 0.64*exp(-5*x/1000)*sin(deg(31.22*x/1000))};
	    							\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-challenge-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the differential equation in terms of <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="challenge-second-example3-diagram" />. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds. Assume that the op-amps are sufficiently sourced such that the outputs will never saturate. The component values are <m>V_{S1} = 0.6~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>V_{S2} = 1.4~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>R_1 = 1~\textrm{k}\Omega</m>, <m>R_{F1} = 2~\textrm{k}\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 2.2~\textrm{k}\Omega</m>, <m>R_{F2} = 4.4~\textrm{k}\Omega</m>, <m>C_1 = 330~\mu\textrm{F}</m>, and <m>C_2 = 470~\mu\textrm{F}</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="challenge-second-example3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-challenge-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-challenge-second-example3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with eight nodes consisting of two op-amps (OA1 and OA2), two voltage sources (Vs1 and Vs2), four resistors (R1, Rf1, R2, and Rf2), two capacitors (C1 and C2), and an SPDT switch. The high-potential end of Vs1 connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. The high-potential end of Vs2 connects to position b of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to R1 at node c. R1, Rf1, C1, and the inverting input of OA1 connect at node d. Rf1, C1, R2, and the output of OA1 connect at node e. R2, Rf2, C2, and the inverting input of OA2 connect at node f. Rf2, C2, and the output of OA2 connect at node g. The low-potential end of both voltage sources and the non-inverting inputs of both op-amps connect to ground at node h. Voltage vX(t) drops from the output of OA1 to ground. Voltage v(t) drops from the output of OA2 to ground. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position a to position b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) node[cute spdt up arrow, xscale=-1, anchor=in] 	(switch) {};
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[below] {b} --++ (left:0.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]	++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[above] {a} --++ (left:2) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$]	++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\node[ground]	at (opamp.+) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]	++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short]	++(up:1) to [C, l^=$C_1$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [short, *-]	++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=$R_{F1}$]	++(right:2.25) -| ($(opamp.out)+(0,1.8)$) to [short, -*]	++(down:0.3);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(2.5,0)$) node[op amp, anchor=-] (opamp2) {};
									\node[ground]	at (opamp2.+) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) node[below] {$v_X(t)$} to [R, l^=$R_2$, *-*]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.-) to [short]	++(up:1) to [C, l^=$C_2$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp2.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)+(0,1)$) to [short, *-]	++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=$R_{F2}$]	++(right:2.25) -| ($(opamp2.out)+(0,1.8)$) to [short, -*]	++(down:0.3);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.out) to [short, *-o]	++(right:0.5) node[right] {$v(t)$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[5.6~\textrm{V} - 4.7~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-0.484t} + 1.5~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-1.515t}\right]~u(t) + 2.4~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in its initial steady-state configuration. Both capacitors can be replaced by opens.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-challenge-example3-figure1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of two op-amps (OA1 and OA2), voltage source Vs1, and four resistors (R1, Rf1, R2, an Rf2). The high-potential end of Vs1 connects to R1 at node a. R1, Rf1, and the inverting input of OA1 connect at node b. Rf1, R2, and the output of OA1 connect at node c. R2, Rf2, and the inverting input of OA2 connect at node d. Rf2 and the output of OA2 connect at node e. The low-potential end of Vs1 and the non-inverting input to both op-amps connect to ground at node f. Voltage vX(0) drops from the output of OA1 to ground. Voltage v(0) drops from the output of OA2 to ground.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\node[ground]	at (opamp.+) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-]	++(left:2.5)to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$]	++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short]	++(up:1) to [R, l^=$R_{F1}$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(2.5,0)$) node[op amp, anchor=-] (opamp2) {};
									\node[ground]	at (opamp2.+) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) node[below] {$v_X(0)$} to [R, l^=$R_2$, *-*]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.-) to [short]	++(up:1) to [R, l^=$R_{F2}$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp2.out);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.out) to [short, *-o]	++(right:0.5) node[right] {$v(0)$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit consists of two cascading inverting op-amps. Use <xref ref="ch4-invertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate <m>v_X(0)</m> and use <xref ref="ch4-cascadedtotaleq" /> and <xref ref="ch4-invertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate <m>v(0)</m>. Both of these voltages are continuous with time.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v_X(0) \amp= (0.6~\textrm{V})\left(-\frac{2~\textrm{K}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (0.6~\textrm{V})(-2)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -1.2~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= (0.6~\textrm{V})\left(-\frac{2~\textrm{K}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)\left(-\frac{4.4~\textrm{K}\Omega}{2.2~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (0.6~\textrm{V})(-2)(-2)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in its final steady-state configuration to calculate the particular solution, <m>v(\infty)</m>. Both capacitors can be replaced by opens.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-challenge-example3-figure2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of two op-amps (OA1 and OA2), voltage source Vs2, and four resistors (R1, Rf1, R2, an Rf2). The high-potential end of Vs2 connects to R1 at node a. R1, Rf1, and the inverting input of OA1 connect at node b. Rf1, R2, and the output of OA1 connect at node c. R2, Rf2, and the inverting input of OA2 connect at node d. Rf2 and the output of OA2 connect at node e. The low-potential end of Vs2 and the non-inverting input to both op-amps connect to ground at node f. Voltage v(infinity) drops from the output of OA2 to ground.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\node[ground]	at (opamp.+) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]	++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short]	++(up:1) to [R, l^=$R_{F1}$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(2.5,0)$) node[op amp, anchor=-] (opamp2) {};
									\node[ground]	at (opamp2.+) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [R, l^=$R_2$, *-*]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.-) to [short]	++(up:1) to [R, l^=$R_{F2}$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp2.out);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.out) to [short, *-o]	++(right:0.5) node[right] {$v(\infty)$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit consists of two cascading inverting op-amps. Use <xref ref="ch4-cascadedtotaleq" /> and <xref ref="ch4-invertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= (1.4~\textrm{V})\left(-\frac{2~\textrm{K}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)\left(-\frac{4.4~\textrm{K}\Omega}{2.2~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (1.4~\textrm{V})(-2)(-2)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.6~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to derive the differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch7-challenge-example3-figure3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with six nodes consisting of two op-amps (OA1 and OA2), voltage source Vs2, two capacitors (C1 and C2), and four resistors (R1, Rf1, R2, an Rf2). The high-potential end of Vs2 connects to R1 at node a. R1, Rf1, C1, and the inverting input of OA1 connect at node b. Rf1, C1, R2, and the output of OA1 connect at node c. R2, Rf2, C2, and the inverting input of OA2 connect at node d. Rf2, C2, and the output of OA2 connect at node e. The low-potential end of Vs2 and the non-inverting input to both op-amps connect to ground at node f. Voltage vX(t) drops from the output of OA1 to ground. Voltage v(t) drops from the output of OA2 to ground.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\node[ground]	at (opamp.+) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]	++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short]	++(up:1) to [C, l^=$C_1$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [short, *-]	++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=$R_{F1}$]	++(right:2.25) -| ($(opamp.out)+(0,1.8)$) to [short, -*]	++(down:0.3);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(2.5,0)$) node[op amp, anchor=-] (opamp2) {};
									\node[ground]	at (opamp2.+) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) node[below] {$v_X(t)$} to [R, l^=$R_2$, *-*]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.-) to [short]	++(up:1) to [C, l^=$C_2$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp2.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp2.-)+(0,1)$) to [short, *-]	++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=$R_{F2}$]	++(right:2.25) -| ($(opamp2.out)+(0,1.8)$) to [short, -*]	++(down:0.3);
									\draw[]	(opamp2.out) to [short, *-o]	++(right:0.5) node[right] {$v(t)$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at the inverting input to the left-hand op-amp to obtain one equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-eq-challenge3-kcl-left">\frac{V_{S2}}{R_1} = -C_1\frac{dv_X(t)}{dt} - \frac{v_X(t)}{R_{F1}}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the inverting input to the right-hand op-amp to obtain the second equation. Solve for <m>v_X(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>\frac{v_X(t)}{R_2} \amp= -C_2\frac{dv(t)}{dt} - \frac{v(t)}{R_{F2}}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-eq-challenge3-kcl-right">v_X(t) \amp= -R_2C_2\frac{dv(t)}{dt} - \frac{R_2}{R_{F2}}v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-eq-challenge3-kcl-right" /> into <xref ref="ch7-eq-challenge3-kcl-left" /> to obtain a differential equation in terms of <m>v(t)</m>. Then, reduce to standard form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{V_{S2}}{R_1} \amp= -C_1\frac{d}{dt}\left(-R_2C_2\frac{dv(t)}{dt} - \frac{R_2}{R_{F2}}v(t)\right) - \frac{1}{R_{F1}}\left(-R_2C_2\frac{dv(t)}{dt} - \frac{R_2}{R_{F2}}v(t)\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= R_2C_1C_2\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{C_1R_2}{R_{F2}}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{C_2R_2}{R_{F1}}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{R_2}{R_{F1}R_{F2}}v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_{S2}}{R_1R_2C_1C_2} \amp= \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{1}{C_2R_{F2}} + \frac{1}{C_1R_{F1}}\right)\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{1}{C_1C_2R_{F1}R_{F2}}\right)v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in component values using base units.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>4.1029 = \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + 1.9987\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + 0.7327v(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-damping-parameter" /> to calculate the damping parameter, and use <xref ref="ch7-eq-resonant-frequency" /> to calculate the resonant frequency.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\alpha \amp= \frac{1.9987}{2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.9994~\textrm{Np/s}</mrow>
							<mrow>\omega_0 \amp= \sqrt{0.7327}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.8560~\textrm{rad/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit is overdamped. Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-roots2" /> to calculate the roots of the characteristic equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>s_1 \amp= -0.9994 + \sqrt{0.9994^2 - 0.8560^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.4836</mrow>
							<mrow>s_2 \amp= -0.9994 - \sqrt{0.9994^2 - 0.8560^2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -1.5152</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-challenge3-kcl-right" /> to calculate <m>v'(0^+)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v'(0^+) \amp= \frac{v_X(0^+) + \frac{R_2}{R_{F2}}v(0^+)}{-R_2C_2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-1.2~\textrm{V} + \frac{2.2~\textrm{k}\Omega}{4.4~\textrm{k}\Omega}(2.4~\textrm{V})}{-(2.2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(470~\mu\textrm{F})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0~\textrm{V/s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over-A1" /> and <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over-A2" /> to calculate the coefficients of the equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>A_1 \amp= \frac{-1.5152(2.4-5.6)}{-1.5152 - (-0.4836)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -4.7~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>A_2 \amp= \frac{0.4836(2.4-5.6)}{-1.5152 - (-0.4836)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug everything into <xref ref="ch7-eq-non-homogeneous-over" /> to obtain the equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[5.6~\textrm{V} - 4.7~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-0.484t} + 1.5~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-1.515t}\right]~u(t) + 2.4~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch7-challenge-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the differential equation in terms of <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="challenge-second-example4-diagram" />. Assume that the op-amp is sufficiently sourced such that the output will never saturate.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="challenge-second-example4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch7-challenge-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch7-challenge-second-example4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with five nodes consisting of an op-amp, three resistors (R1, R2, and Rf), and two capacitors (C1 and C2). R1 connects to voltage signal vin(t) at node a. R1, R2, C1, and C2 connect at node b. C1, Rf, and the inverting input to the op-amp connect at node c. C2, Rf, and the output of the op-amp connect at node d. R2 and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect to ground at node e. Voltage vX(t) drops from node b to ground. Voltage v(t) drops from the output of the op-amp to ground.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\node[ground]	at (opamp.+) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [C, l_=$C_1$, *-*]	++(left:2.5) node[above right] {$v_X(t)$} to [R, l_=$R_1$, -o]	++(left:2.5) node[left] {$v_{in}(t)$};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=$R_2$]	++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short]	++(up:1) to [R, l^=$R_F$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) --++(up:2) to [C, l^=$C_2$]	++(right:4.5) -| ($(opamp.out)+(0,2)$) to [short, -*]	++(down:0.5);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-o]	++(right:0.5) node[right] {$v(t)$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>-\frac{1}{R_1C_2}\frac{dv_{in}}{dt} = \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{1}{C_1R_F}+\frac{1}{C_2R_F}\right)\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{1}{C_1C_2R_1R_F}+\frac{1}{C_1C_2R_2R_F}\right)v(t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Perform KCL at the node connecting <m>R_1</m>, <m>R_2</m>, <m>C_1</m>, and <m>C_2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch7-challenge4-opamp-kcl1">\frac{v_{in}(t) - v_X(t)}{R_1} = \frac{v_X(t)}{R_2} + C_2 \frac{d}{dt}\left(v_X(t) - v(t)\right) + C_1\frac{dv_X(t)}{dt}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KCL at the inverting input of the op-amp. Then, solve for <m>v_X(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>C_1\frac{dv_X(t)}{dt} \amp= \frac{-v(t)}{R_F}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{dv_X(t)}{dt} \amp= \frac{-v(t)}{C_1R_F}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch7-challenge4-opamp-kcl2">v_X(t) \amp= -\frac{1}{C_1R_F}\int v(t) \,dt</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch7-challenge4-opamp-kcl2" /> into <xref ref="ch7-challenge4-opamp-kcl1" /> and reduce until the differential equation is in standard form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>-\frac{v_{in}(t)}{R_1} \amp= \frac{1}{R_1C_1R_F}\int v(t) \,dt + \frac{1}{R_2C_1R_F}\int v(t) \,dt + \frac{C_2}{C_1R_F}v(t) + C_2 \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{C_1}{C_1R_F}v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>-\frac{1}{R_1}\frac{dv_{in}}{dt} \amp= \frac{1}{C_1R_1R_F}v(t) + \frac{1}{C_1R_2R_F}v(t) + \frac{C_2}{C_1R_F}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + C_2\frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \frac{1}{R_F}\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>-\frac{1}{R_1C_2}\frac{dv_{in}}{dt} \amp= \frac{d^2v(t)}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{1}{C_1R_F}+\frac{1}{C_2R_F}\right)\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{1}{C_1C_2R_1R_F}+\frac{1}{C_1C_2R_2R_F}\right)v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			
			
		</subexercises>

	</exercises>

</chapter>